<H1>V‰RACû™IYAM (T.V. Gangadharan's translation)</H1>

<P>Master file kept in "...\UTF2PDY\VIRACO\VC_TRSL.PDY"
<BR>Latest modification: 2005/april/5

<H1>Prolegomenon (P‡yiram)</H1>


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_0a">The Special Preface relating to the Invocation and the Theme</A></H3>


<P>Bearing on his crown the blessed feet of Lord Buddha the lofty one, the one enthroned under the Bodhi tree, the one endowed with multi-foliate powers acquired through asceticism, the one who by reason of his great excellence resulting from asceticism is not to be matched by any one, the one who is to be counted pure in mind and word and body he has essayed to incite the grammar on Tamil. He is indeed Buddha Mitthiram, the prince of the realm of Ponpaüüi grit with green gardens. [The author here speaks of himself in the third person.] 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_0b">The Apology (Avai-y-aÒakkam)</A></H3>


<P>If I am questioned: "Can you speak on that Tamil grammar, the work wrought for the benefit of them that live in the world where abides his immortal fame, the work which is so cooling to the mind, the work of Akattiya≠ who is endowed with clarified intellect, the work of the chõla (pupil) of Avalùka≠ whose cultural attainment is hailed by many discerning scholars", I will answer thus: "The fly too wings the spacious sky expanse where flies the Brahminy kite!  In this is the fitness of things? What does the world say?  (Does the world approve of this?) If this can pass muster, so can my work also."


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_0c">The Name of the Work</A></H3>


<P>Tamil grammar deals with the vast pentad of letters, word, subject-matter (<i>poruÎ</i>), prosody and figures (orthography, etymology, poetics, poetry and rhetoric) which abide in the tongues of poets and which are the essential parts of poems. Briefly have I treated these indicating here and there the ancient rules of Sanskrit grammar too. As V„racù©a≠ whose chariot in decked with flower garlands has caused this to be written, I christen this V„racù©iyam. 

<HR>

<H1>V‰RACû™IYAM, First Part: The Section on Letters:</H1>

<H2>The Chapter on the combination of letters.</H2>

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_1">Vi_1</A>
<BR>((On the triad of vowels, consonants and ‡ytham))</H3>


<P>1. If the known and articulated letters, the twelve beginning with  <i>a</i> and ending with <i>au</i> are called vowels and the eighteen beginning with <i>k</i> and ending with <i>≠</i> are called consonants. That letter which is sui generis and poised midway between the vowels and the consonants is called the ‡ytham. In the 18 consonants the six letters <i>y r l v © Î</i> are medial consonants, the six letters: <i>Ô § ı n m</i> and <i>≠</i> are soft consonants and the remaining six letters: <i>k c Ò t p</i> and <i>ü</i> are harsh consonants. 

 
<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_2">Vi_2</>
<BR>((The short and long vowel-consonants, the vowel elongation, relative letters and the over-short <i>u</i>.))</H3>


<P>2.
<BR>Vowel-consonants which have a consonant as the end-letter are arranged in eighteen strands. In each strand of the vowel-consonants devoid of the consonant, five are designated "short letters" and seven as "long letters". The vowel elongation is a still further elongation of the long letter. Hard and soft consonants in combination come out in pairs and are known as "relative letters". At the end of a long letter or a word made up of several letters, if the letter <i>u</i> unites with the six hard consonants <i>k c Ò t p</i> and <i>ü</i> that <i>u</i> gets shortened and is known as "over-short <i>u</i>". 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_3">Vi_3</A>
<BR>((<i>au</i> and <i>ai</i>; interrogative letters, letters which get dotted, demonstrative letters and the over-short <i>u</i>.))</H3>


<P>3.
<BR><i>au</i> results when <i>a</i> unites with <i>u</i>; when <i>a</i> unites with <i>i, ai</i> is produced. The letters <i>‡ õ</i> and <i>ù</i> imply interrogation only when they form the end-letters. <i>õ</i> and <i>ù</i> as well as the 18 consonants are known as dotted letters.[1] The letters <i>a i</i> and <i>u,</i> when employed demonstratively at the beginning of a word, are called demonstrative letters. When the over-short <i>u</i> combines with <i>y</i> as the initial letters of the juxtapaused word, the over-short <i>u</i> becomes <i>i</i>. That <i>i</i> gets dwindled in its <i>m‡ttirai</i> and is called the over-short <i>i</i>.


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_4">Vi_4</A>
<BR>((Interrogative letters, coalescing of two mute consonants and coalescing of three mute consonants))</H3>


<P>4. The letters <i>e õ</i> and <i>y‡</i>- which imply interrogation come as the first letters. The letters getting born by reason of fourfold accent --- loud, soft middling and whooping and undergoing coalescing of two mute consonants and occasionally three mute consonants as the case may be, when conjoined with themselves or other consonants. Thus say the wise. 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_5">Vi_5</A>
<BR>((The <i>m‡ttirais</i> of the letters and the conjunction of the first vowel of the succeeding word with the last consonant of the preceding word))</H3>


<P>5. The time-measures of the letters are as follows: 
<BR>(1) Short vowel ... 1 
<BR>(2) Long vowel ... 2 
<BR>(3) Among these, for <i>ai</i> and <i>au</i> ... 1+(1/2) 
<BR>(4) Consonant, <i>‡ytham</i>, over-short <i>i</i> and over-short <i>u</i> ... (1/2) 
<BR>(5) Prolonged vowels ... 3 
<BR>(6) The measure of the vowel-consonant is the measure of its vowel (only). 
<BR>The vowel which forms the first letter of the succeeding word will combine with the consonant, that is, the last letter of the preceding word, becoming in the process a vowel-consonant. 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_6">Vi_6</A>
<BR>((The places of the genesis of letters and the effort needed therefore))</H3>


<P>6. The wind rising from the navel courses through chest, head, throat and nose. When it gets expressed, it assumes different sounds by reasons of the activation of palate, teeth, tongue and lips. Hard consonants, <i>‡ytham</i>, medial consonants and vowels as well as soft consonants get born respectively in chest, head, throat and nose. 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_7">Vi_7</A>
<BR>((The Initial letters))</H3>


<P>7. The twelve vowels, all the vowel-consonants of <i>ka ca ta na pa</i> and <i>ma</i>, eight vowel-consonants of <i>va</i>, six vowel-consonants of <i>ya</i> are the initial letters of the Tamil words. 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_8">Vi_8</A>
<BR>((Finals))</H3>


<P>8. Of soft consonants the letters <i>≠</i>, <i>m</i> and <i>ı</i>, of medial consonants the five letters other than <i>v</i> and of vowels the ten letters other than <i>e</i> and <i>o</i> are the finals of Tamil words. 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_9">Vi_9</A>
<BR>((Natural combination and mutational combination))</H3>



<P>9. When the first letter of the succeeding word combines with the last letter of the receding word, any one of the following may result:

<BR>(1) Natural combination. 

<BR>(2) Insertion. 

<BR>(3) Transmutation and 

<BR>(4) Omission. 

<BR>All the three results (2 - 4) are sometimes noticeable in single words also. 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_10">Vi_10</A>
<BR>((Mutational combinations according to Sanskrit))</H3>

 

<P>10. Mutational combinations may occur (in Sanskrit) in a single word or letter. There are therefore six combinations. Again, as the occurrence may be in the beginning or he middle or the end, eighteen. These changes are according to usage only.  

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_11">Vi_11</A>
<BR>((The changes occurring to the letter <i>na</i> which conveys the negative sense and the changes to the letters <i>õ</i> and <i>ù</i> in Sanskrit))</H3>

 

<P>11.       In Sanskrit, if before the letter the negative <i>na</i> a word beginning with a vowel comes, <i>na</i> becomes <i>an</i>; it becomes <i>a</i> in the case of a consonant. <i>õ</i> and <i>ù</i> of Sanskrit become <i>ai</i> and <i>au</i> in Tamil. 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_12">Vi_12</A>
<BR>((The combinations in Sanskrit))</H3>

 

<P>12. <i>Virutthi</i> and <i>guıam</i> are the combinations in Sanskrit. In noun-roots and verb-roots <i>a</i> becomes <i>‡</i>, <i>i</i> becomes <i>ai,</i> <i>u</i> becomes <i>au</i> and <i>Á</i> becomes <i>‡r</i>. These changes are known as <i>virutthi</i>. Similarly, when <i>i</i> becomes <i>e</i> and <i>u</i> becomes <i>o</i> the changes are known as <i>guıam</i>. 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_13">Vi_13</A>
<BR>((The combinations in Tamil --- vowel before vowel and instances of omission --- relating to last letters of preceding words))</H3>

 

<P>13.       If preceding words ending in <i>i „</i> and <i>ai</i> are combined with succeeding words beginning with a vowel, the consonantal glide comes into being. In the case of other words <i>v</i> is the gliding consonant. In the case of <i>õ</i> alone, either <i>y</i> or <i>v</i> will be the gliding consonant. When succeeding words combine with preceding words, omission of a syllable or a vowel-consonant or a vowel or a consonant may occur. 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_14">Vi_14</A>
<BR>((The joining of <i>t</i> and <i>n</i> which are the first letters of a succeeding word with <i>ı</i>, the last letter of the preceding word; the joining of the vowel which is the first letter of the succeeding word with the last letter, a consonant following a short vowel or vowel-consonant; and the joining of hard consonants of the succeeding word with <i>y r ©</i> of the preceding word))</H3>

 

<P>14.       In the above cases when <i>t</i> combines with <i>ı</i> the former changes into <i>ı</i>. When to first letter of the succeeding word which is a vowel combines with the last letter (consonant) of the preceding word made up of two letters, namely, a short vowel or vowel-consonant and a consonant (only), then the consonant at the end gets doubled. When the hard consonant (the first letter) of the succeeding word joins with is <i>y r</i> <i>©</i> --- the end letters --- of the preceding word, either the hard consonant or its corresponding soft consonant gets inserted between the two words. 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_15">Vi_15</A>
<BR>((<i>n</i> joining with <i>i „ ai</i> of the preceding word; <i>t</i> joining words ending in <i>©</i> and <i>≠</i>))</H3>

 

<P>15.       When <i>n</i> (the first letter) combines with the end-letters <i>i „ ai</i> of the preceding word, <i>n</i> changes into <i>§.</i> When <i>t</i> joins <i>©</i> or <i>≠</i> of the preceding word, both may change into <i>Ò</i>; sometimes <i>©</i> or <i>≠</i> may get omitted.[2] 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_16">Vi_16</A>
<BR>((Hard consonants and <i>n</i> (the first letter) of the succeeding word joining the end-letter <i>≠</i> of the preceding word; <i>≠</i> preceded by a single long vowel or long vowel-consonant joining <i>t</i> (the first letter) of the succeeding word))</H3>

 

<P>16.      When a hard consonant joins <i>≠</i> the end-letter of the preceding word, <i>≠</i> becomes <i>Ò</i>; when <i>n</i> combines with the end-letter <i>≠</i> of the preceding word, <i>≠</i> becomes <i>ı</i> [This is for case terms (<i>võüüamai-p-puıarcci</i>)] When <i>≠</i> preceded by a long vowel or long vowel-consonant is joined by t<i>,</i> <i>n</i> becomes <i>Ò</i> and <i>≠</i> gets omitted. [This is for non-case terms (<i>alvali-p-puıarcci</i>).] 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_17">Vi_17</A>
<BR>((Hard consonants, <i>n</i> and <i>y</i> joining <i>l</i> the end-letter of the preceding word; <i>n</i> joining the end-letter, <i>y</i>))</H3>

 

<P>17.       When a hard consonant which is the first letter of the succeeding word joins the end-letter <i>l</i> of the preceding word, <i>l</i> becomes <i>ü.</i> When the hard consonant is <i>t</i>, it also becomes <i>ü.</i> When <i>n</i> joins the end-letter <i>l</i> of the preceding word, both become <i>≠</i>. When it joins the end-word <i>l</i> of the preceding word, the particle <i>i</i> springs in between. When the first letter <i>n</i> of the succeeding word joins the end-letter <i>y</i>, <i>n</i> becomes <i>§</i>. 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_18">Vi_18</A>
<BR>((<i>©</i> joining hard consonants, <i>n</i> and <i>m</i>; natural combination))</H3>

 

<P>18.       When <i>©</i> joins a hard consonant, <i>©</i> becomes <i>ı</i> or <i>Ò</i>. When it joins <i>n</i>, it gets changed into <i>ı</i>. When it joins <i>m,</i> it becomes <i>ı.</i> In natural combination no change occurs.[3] 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_19">Vi_19</A>
<BR>((<i>m</i> joining hard and soft consonants; <i>m</i> joining <i>v</i>; <i>l</i> joining <i>t</i>))</H3>

 

<P> 19.      When <i>m</i> joins a hard consonant which is the first letter of the succeeding word, <i>m</i> changes into that hard consonant or its corresponding soft consonant. When <i>m</i> as end-letter of the preceding word joins soft consonants, <i>m</i> gets omitted. When <i>m</i> joins <i>v</i>, <i>m</i> gets shortened in sound to the extent of {{1/4}} <i>m‡ttirai</i>. The shortened <i>m</i> is to be inscribed with yet another dot inside the semicircular part of the letter <i>m</i>. When <i>l</i> as last letter is preceded by a long vowel/long vowel-consonant or two or three letters combines with the first letter <i>t</i> of the succeeding word, <i>l</i> gets omitted and <i>t</i> becomes <i>ü</i>. 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_20">Vi_20</A>
<BR>((<i>≠</i> joining <i>t</i> and <i>≠</i>; vowel joining hard consonant))</H3>

 

<P>20.       When the end-letter <i>≠</i> joins the first letter <i>n, n</i> becomes <i>≠</i>; when <i>≠</i> joins <i>t, t</i> becomes <i>ü</i>. When vowels join hard consonants, either those hard consonants or their corresponding soft consonants spring up between the words. 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_21">Vi_21</A>
<BR>((Over-short <i>u</i> joining vowels standing first of the succeeding word; perfect <i>u</i> joining vowel; over-short <i>u</i> joining hard consonant))</H3>



<P>21.       When the end-letter, over-short <i>u</i> joins the first letter, a vowel of the succeeding word, the over-short <i>u</i> disappears leaving its consonant intact to join the vowel. When the over-short <i>u</i> joins a hard consonant, the soft consonant preceding the over-short <i>u</i> becomes hard and the hard consonant which is the initial letter of the succeeding (word) gets doubled. Occasionally the perfect <i>u</i> also when it joins the initial letter which is a vowel of the succeeding word, may disappear leaving its consonant intact to join the vowel. 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_22">Vi_22</A>
<BR>((<i>l</i> joining <i>m</i> and <i>n</i>; <i>≠</i> and <i>ı</i> joining hard consonants; <i>©</i> joining a vowel))</H3>

 

<P>22.       When the end-letter <i>l</i> of the preceding word joins <i>m</i> and <i>n, l</i> becomes <i>≠</i>. When <i>≠</i> joins a hard consonant which the initial letter of the succeeding word, <i>≠</i> becomes <i>ü</i>, when <i>ı</i> joins a hard consonant <i>ı</i> becomes <i>Ò</i>. When the word <i>n‡©i</i> joins the word <i>uri</i>, the resulting compound is <i>n‡Òuri</i>. 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_23">Vi_23</A>
<BR>((The changing forms of numbers in combination))</H3>

 

<P>23.              <i>o≠üu</i> becomes <i>oru</i> and <i>ùr</i>, <i>iraıÒu</i> becomes <i>iru</i> and <i>„r</i>, <i>mÂ≠üu,</i>  <i>mu</i> and <i>mÂ</i>, <i>n‡lu</i> becomes <i>n‡≠ku</i>, <i>aintu</i> <i>ai</i>,  <i>‡üu</i>  <i>aüu</i>, <i>õ©</i> <i>e©u</i>, <i>eÒÒu</i> <i>eı</i>, <i>o≠patu</i> <i>toı</i> and <i>toı</i>, <i>pattu</i> <i>p‡≠</i>, <i>pa≠Âüu,</i> <i>patu</i> and <i>p‡©tu</i>. <i>pa≠≠Âüu</i> becomes <i>‡yiram</i>.  

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_24">Vi_24</A>
<BR>((<i>§, n, m, v</i> joining <i>a i u e; y</i> joining <i>a, i, u, e; Ò</i> becoming <i>ı</i>; the appearing of <i>ü</i> in some places; <i>‡</i> at the end becoming <i>a</i> and joining <i>u</i>))</H3>



<P> 24.      When <i>§, n, m</i> and <i>v</i> join the demonstrative and interrogative particles, they are preceded by the self-same consonants. If it is preceded by <i>a, i, u</i> and <i>e, v</i> appears between the two words. (<i>võÒkai + av‡ = võıav‡; mu≠ + il = mu≠üil</i>. The words ending in <i>‡</i> (<i>nil‡</i>) get shortened as <i>a</i> (<i>nila</i>) and will sometimes combine with <i>u</i> (<i>nilavu</i>).} 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_25">Vi_25</A>
<BR>((Certain rare changes in combination))</H3>

 

<P>25.       When a vowel or vowel-consonant joins the consonant of a preceding word, in some instances, the end-consonant may disappear lengthening the preceding <i>a</i>. In some instance the initial letter of the succeeding word may disappear. There are rare instances where <i>pùlum</i> becomes <i>pùlm/pù≠m</i> and <i>maruÎum</i> becomes <i>maruım. </i> In some instances <i>m</i> may be substituted by <i>≠</i> at the end of a noun. 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_26">Vi_26</A>
<BR>((Changes occurring to demonstrative pronouns))</H3>

 

<P> 26.      <i>atu, itu</i> and <i>utu</i> become <i>a´tu</i>, <i>i´tu</i> and <i>u´tu, atu.</i> + <i>a≠üu</i> will become <i>at‡≠üu</i>. In some cases, <i>aÔku, iÔku</i> and <i>uÔku</i> will become <i>‡Ôku, „Ôku</i> and <i>ÂÔku</i>. In some cases the prolongation of the long vowel may occur. 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_27">Vi_27</A>
<BR>((Changes occurring when a word joins a case-ending and in case combinations))</H3>

 

<P>27.       When a noun combines with a case-ending, and in case combinations a euphonic increment comes into being in the middle; the last consonant and sometimes the vowel preceding it and sometimes the last vowel-consonant may get omitted in the preceding word. 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_28">Vi_28</A>
<BR>((<i>≠</i> and <i>l</i> joining <i>t</i>; supplementary rules for this chapter))</H3>

 

<P>28. When <i>≠</i> or <i>l</i> combines with <i>t, t</i> changes into <i>Ò</i> and <i>ü</i> respectively. <i>≠</i> and <i>l</i> may change into <i>‡ytham</i>. Whatever is omitted without enunciation in this chapter may be made good with available materials. 

 

 

<HR>

<H1>The Section on Words</H1>



<H2>The chapter relating to cases [29-37]</H2>

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_29">Vi_29</A>
<BR>((The group of cases and the group of <i>k‡rakams</i>))</H3>

 

<P>29.  Beginning with the nominative there are eight-fold cases of noun. [<i>k‡rakam</i> covers nouns with case-endings having a syntactical link with verbs.] 

<P><i>K‡rakams</i> are six in number. They are (1) the nominative (2) the objective (3) the instrumental (4) the dative (5) the ablative and (6) the locative. 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_30">Vi_30</A>
<BR>((The classification of nouns and the case-endings of the nominative case))</H3>

 

<P>30.       Nouns are eightfold. They relate to (1) male singular (2) female singular (3) plural (4) neuter singular (5) sentient plural (6) honorific singular (7) honorific plural and (8) honorific neuter singular. [[Transl. of commentary]]

<P>The case-endings of the nominative case are the seven particles which are <i>r</i>, <i>ar</i>, <i>‡r</i>, <i>arkaÎ</i>, <i>‡rkaÎ</i>, <i>kaÎ</i> and <i>m‡r</i>. These particles can be retained even when the other case-endings are combined with them. (2) [[Transl. of sÂtra]]

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_31">Vi_31</A>
<BR>((The 64 <i>k‡raka</i> words))</H3>

 

<P>31.       The aforesaid eight roots (<i>pirakiruti</i>) with their combination of the case-endings which are eightfold yield 64 <i>k‡raka</i> words in Tamil. (3) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_32">Vi_32</A>
<BR>((The <i>k‡raka</i> words in Sankskrit))</H3>

 

<P>32. In Sanskrit there are three genders: the male, the female and the neuter. There are seven case-endings from first to seventh. In numbers there are three categories: singular, dual and plural. Nouns are twofold, the vowel-ending and the consonant-ending. If these are multiplied (3x7x3x2) we get 126 <i>k‡raka</i> words. 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_33">Vi_33</A>
<BR>((The nominative case-ending joiningthe eightfold root))</H3>

 

<P>33.      <i>cu, ar, ‡r, arkaÎ, ‡rkaÎ, kaÎ</i> and <i>m‡r</i>; these seven are the nominative case-endings. The masculine singular the feminine singular and the neuter singular will have <i>cu</i> as the case-ending. In neuter plural <i>cu</i> and <i>kaÎ</i> will be the two case-endings. The case-endings of the honorific masculine and feminine singulars will cover as instances save <i>kaÎ</i>. In all places the particle <i>cu</i> will disappear when the noun joins a word. (5) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_34">Vi_34</A>
<BR>(( Case-endings of cases 2 to 4 and what they mean))</H3>

 

<P> 34.      <i>ai</i> in the case-ending of the second case and in the objective case. The omission of this case-ending will not alter the meaning, generally. The case-endings of the third case are <i>oÒu</i>, <i>oÒu</i> and <i>‡l</i>. These stand for the subject or the instrument. The case-endings of the fourth case are <i>ku</i> and <i>poruÒÒu</i>. The fourth case is also the dative case. (6) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_35">Vi_35</A>
<BR>((The cases 5, 6 and 7))))</H3>

 

<P>35.       The case-ending of the fifth case is <i>ni≠üu</i> which combines with the case-ending of the seventh case, namely, <i>il</i>. It means limit.

<P>The sixth case is the possessive/ genitive case. Its case-endings are: <i>uÒaiyi≠</i>, <i>uÒaiy‡r</i>, <i>uÒaiy‡rkaÎ</i>, <i>uÒaiyatu</i> and <i>uÒaiyi≠a</i>. <i>uÒaiya</i> is also included sometimes.

<P>The sixth case is not a <i>k‡rakam</i>. If it is to be governed by a verb, <i>ku</i> will be the case-ending. The seventh is the locative case. Its meaning is base or support. Its case-endings are <i>kõ</i>, <i>u©ai</i>, <i>vayi≠</i>, etc. (7) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_36">Vi_36</A>
<BR>((The case-endings of the vocative case))</H3>

 

<P>36.       While the other cases are common for the First, Second and Third Persons, vocative case refers only to the Second Person. Its case-endings are <i>‡y</i>, <i>‡Î</i>, <i>„</i>, <i>õ</i>, <i>a</i>, <i>‡</i>, <i>‡≠</i>, <i>ùl</i>, <i>ùy</i> and <i>„r</i>. In some words the last consonant gets omitted (in this case). (8) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_37">Vi_37</A>
<BR>((Certain roots undergoing change in the nominative case and the demonstrative and the interrogative roots becoming subjects after combining with terminations))</H3>

 

<P>37.       From the roots <i>u≠</i>, <i>e≠</i> and <i>ta≠</i> are derived <i>n„</i>, <i>n‡</i> and <i>t‡≠</i> respectively, the latter being the nominative case. The roots <i>a, i, u</i> and <i>y‡</i> --- each combining with the termination <i>va≠, vaÎ, var, varkaÎ, tu</i> and <i>vai</i> will form words of various genders and numbers. (9) 

 
<HR>

<H2>The chapter relating to <i>k‡rakam</i> [38-43]</H2>

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_38">Vi_38</A>
<BR>(())</H3>

<P>38. Nouns with case-endings governed by verbs are <i>k‡rakam</i>. These are six in number. The doer of an action is the subject. The action or the result of the action is the object. That which is used for the performance of action is the instrument. The removal of a thing from something is <i>avati</i>. The place of an action is location. The receiver of a thing is <i>kùÎi</i>. (1) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_39">Vi_39</A>
<BR>((Examples for the <i>k‡rakams</i>))</H3>

 

<P>39. "A Brahmin having come down from a hill gathered with his hands fragrant flowers from a tank in which such flowers teemed and with a view to do away with his sin offered them to the Blemishless one."

 
<UL>
<LI>Brahmin: Nominative <i>k‡rakam.</i>
<LI>gathered ... flowers : Objective <i>k‡rakam</i>. 
<LI>with his hands: Instrumental <i>k‡rakam</i>. 
<LI>offered to the Blemishless One: Dative <i>k‡rakam</i>. 
<LI>having come down from a hill: Ablative <i>k‡rakam</i>. 
<LI>tank in which such flowers teemed: Locative <i>k‡rakam</i>. 
</UL>
(2) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_40">Vi_40</A>
& <A NAME="_Vi_trsl_41">Vi_41</A>
<BR>(( How the six <i>k‡rakams</i> turn into 23 in all))</H3>

 

<P>40&41. The nominative <i>k‡rakam</i> is of five types:
<UL>
<LI>(1) <i>k‡raıa karutt‡</i> (This emphasises the fact that the nominative is very essential for the action.) 

<LI>(2) <i>t‡≠ teri karutt‡</i> (The nominative is clearly revealed by its case-ending <i>‡l</i>.)

<LI>(3) <i>t‡≠ teriy‡k karutt‡</i> (Here the nominative is not easily comprehended. It is done with the help of the predicate and the object.) 

<LI>(4)<i> karuma karutt‡</i> (An instance of the object becoming the subject.) 

<LI>(5) <i>talaimaik karutt‡</i> (An instance of the sole self-responsible etc. for action and its result.) 
</UL>

<P><i>avati</i> is of two kinds: (1) movable and (2) immovable,

<P>Instrumental <i>k‡rakam</i> is of two kinds: (1) separable and (2) inseparable. 

<P>Dative <i>k‡rakam</i> is of three types: (1) the enthusiastic (2) the indifferent and (3) the alms-seeking. 

<P>Objective <i>k‡rakam</i> is of seven kinds: 

<UL>
<LI>(1) <i>paüüuk karumam</i> (attachment to the object.) 

<LI>(2) <i>v„ÒÒuk karumam</i> (abandonment of the object.) 

<LI>(3) <i>irupuüak karumam</i> (both attachment and abandonment of the object. 

<LI>(4) <i>t‡≠ teri karumam</i> (the noticeability of the object.) 

<LI>(5) <i>t‡≠ teriy‡k karumam</i> (the veiling of the object.) 

<LI>(6) <i>karutt‡ karumam</i> (the object becoming the subject.) 

<LI>(7) <i>t„pakat tuvi karumam</i> (presence of two objects with case-ending
</UL>

<P>locative <i>k‡rakam</i> is of four kinds: 
<UL>
<LI>(1) <i>cõrvu ‡t‡ram</i> (seat of support.) 

<LI>(2) <i>kalappu ‡t‡ram</i> (pervasion of support.) 

<LI>(3) <i>pula≠ ‡t‡ram</i> (natural habitation.) 

<LI>(4) <i>ayal ‡t‡ram</i> (temporary habitation.) (3), (4)
</UL>
 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_42">Vi_42</A>
& <A NAME="_Vi_trsl_43">Vi_43</A>
<BR>((The case-endings of the seven cases applying multifariously to the 23 <i>k‡rakams</i></H3>


<P>42&43. Among the <i>karutt‡</i>s excluding <i>t‡≠ teriy‡k karutt‡</i> which is restricted to nominative case alone, in all others, the first, third and sixth cases come to play multifariously, <i>t‡≠ teriy‡k karumam</i> is restricted to the nominative case only. In others the second, third, fourth and sixth case-endings come to play multifariously. 

<UL>
<LI>For <i>k‡raıakk‡rakam</i> the third, fifth and sixth come to play variously. 
<LI>For <i>kùÎikk‡rakam</i> the third, fourth and sixth have multiform operation. 
<LI>The <i>avatikk‡rakam</i> is restricted to the fifth case and the <i>‡t‡rakk‡rakam</i> to the seventh. (5), (6) 
</UL>

<HR>

<H2>The chapter on ellipsis [44-51]</H2>

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_44">Vi_44</A>
<BR>((definition of ellipsis; the case ellipsis and its kinds</H3>

 

<P>44. Ellipsis is the combination of two or more nouns resulting in a compound word. In case ellipsis the case-endings get omitted without <i>jeopardy</i> to the meaning of the compound word. In case ellipsis many are the instances where only the case-endings get omitted; in a few other instances the omission relates to the case-endings as well as the governing verbs. There are also certain very rare instances where besides the case-endings and their corresponding governing verbs, some other nouns too get omitted. (1) 

 
<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_45">Vi_45</A>
<BR>((The six kinds of ellipsis in Sanskrit))</H3>

 
<P>45.
<UL>
<LI>(1) <i>tatpuru˘a≠</i> (case ellipsis.)  It is of eight kinds, namely, case-endings 1 to 7 and <i>na§§u</i> (the case-ending which conveys the opposite sense). 
<LI>(2) <i>pahuvir„ki</i> (metonymical ellipsis) 
<LI>(3) <i>karmat‡raya≠</i> (adjectival ellipsis) 
<LI>(4) <i>tuvantuva≠</i> (cumulative ellipsis) 
<LI>(5) <i>tviku</i> (number ellipsis) 
<LI>(6) <i>avyayip‡vam</i> (particle ellipsis) (2) 
</UL>
 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_46">Vi_46</A>
<BR>((Case ellipsis and number ellipsis))</H3>

 

<P>46. Case ellipsis is of eight kinds. Vide 45. In number ellipsis, the number comes as the preceding word followed by a noun. It is of two kinds: singular and plural. (3) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_47">Vi_47</A>
<BR>((Metonymical ellipsis and its kinds))</H3>


<P>47. This is of seven kinds:
<UL>
<LI>(1)Ellipsis in two words, 
<LI>(2) Ellipsis in many words, 
<LI>(3) Ellipsis having a number in the succeeding word, 
<LI>(4) Ellipsis having a number in both the preceding and succeeding words, 
<LI>(5) Ellipsis where a word is repeated, 
<LI>(6) Ellipsis where the word <i>caka</i> comes as the preceding word, 
<LI>(7) Ellipsis relating to directions. 
</UL>
<P>In metonymical ellipsis the meaning of the resulting compound word abides beyond that word (4) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_48">Vi_48</A>
<BR>((Adjectival ellipsis and its kinds))</H3>

 

<P>48. This is of six kinds.
<UL>
<LI>(1) The preceding word is an adjective of quality.
<LI>(2) The succeeding word refers to quality.
<LI>(3) The preceding word is a comparison.
<LI>(4) The succeeding word is a comparison.
<LI>(5) The preceding word refers to intention.
<LI>(6) The preceding word refers to certainty. (5) 
</UL>
 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_49">Vi_49</A>
<BR>((The particle ellipsis and the cumulative ellipsis))</H3>

 

<P>49. The particle ellipsis is of two kinds: (1) The particle being the preceding word, (2) The particle being the succeeding word. 

<P>The cumulative ellipsis is of two kinds: (1) Referring to the personal class, (2) referring to the impersonal class. (6) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_50">Vi_50</A>
<BR>((The six ellipses in Tamil and the kinds of adjectival and cumulative ellipses))</H3>

 

<P>50. The elliptical compounds are of six kinds, namely, case-related, verbal, adjectival, comparative, cumulative and metonymical. 

<P>In verbal ellipsis a verb-root joins a noun to form a compound word. 

<P>In comparative ellipsis the preceding word comprises the comparison and the succeeding word refers to the compared. 

<P>Adjectival ellipsis comprises five kinds; these have reference to quantity, form, colour, taste and quality. 

<P>Cumulative ellipsis is of six kinds; these refer to (1) two words, (2) many words, (3) measures, (4) weights, (5) numbers of impersonal classes, and (6) [numbers] of personal classes. (7) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_51">Vi_51</A>
<BR>((Loss of letters/syllables/words in the six kinds of ellipses; the signification of compounds))</H3>

 

<P>51.  In metonymical ellipsis and other ellipses sometimes the last or the middle word gets omitted. In some instances syllables and letters too get omitted. 

<P>The signification of each compound is indicated by the first or the second word, or by both (in the compound) or else by the other words (suppressed at the end). (8) 


<HR>

<H2>The chapter on appellative noun-roots. [52-59]</H2>

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_52">Vi_52</A>
<BR>((Some common terminations of the noun-roots))</H3>

 

<P>52.  <i>a≠, iya≠, „≠a≠</i>, etc. form the particles which are the terminations of the noun-roots. (1) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_53">Vi_53</A>
& <A NAME="_Vi_trsl_54">Vi_54</A>
<BR>((Operation of <i>tattitam</i>))</H3>

 

<P>53&54. The instances where <i>tattitam</i> operates: 
<UL>
<LI>(1) <i>uııum ita≠‡l</i> (lives by this), 
<LI>(2) <i>utaikkum ita≠ai</i> (chants this),  
<LI>(3) <i>uÒaiya≠ itu</i> (possesses this), 
<LI>(4) <i>paııum ita≠ai</i> (makes this), 
<LI>(5) <i>payilum itil</i> (practises this), 
<LI>(6) <i>paya≠koÎÎum ita≠ai</i> (benefits by this), 
<LI>(7) <i>eııum ita≠ai</i> (counts this), 
<LI>(8) <i>ita≠ukku n‡yaka≠</i> (monarch of this kingdom), 
<LI>(9) <i>„Ôkirukkum</i> (abides here), 
<LI>(10) <i>naııum ita≠ai</i> (attain this), 
<LI>(11) <i>itai okkum</i> (resembles this), 
<LI>(12) <i>iÔkuÎa≠</i> [(He) is here], 
<LI>(13) <i>maka≠ iva≠ukku</i> (son of him), 
<LI>(14) <i>maka≠ ivaÎukku</i> (son of her), 
<LI>(15) <i>inta varkkatti≠a≠</i> (of this race) 
<LI>and (16) <i>iva≠ait takaviya tõva≠a e≠p‡≠</i> (worshipper of this deity). 
</UL>

<P>The list is truly endless. (2), (3) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_55">Vi_55</A>
<BR>((Terminations are abstract and (some) common nouns))</H3>

<P>55. When terminations <i>mai, am</i>, etc. join quality-roots (that are <i>vali, neÒu</i>, etc.) abstract nouns are formed, when terminations <i>ma≠ u≠</i>, etc. join quality-roots (some of) the common nouns are formed. (4) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_56">Vi_56</A>
<BR>((Terminations denoting feminine gender; certain changes who Sanskrit words are introduced in Tamil))</H3>

<P>56. When terminations denoting feminine gender that are <i>acci, ‡ÒÒi</i>, etc. join nouns which denote caste, quality occupation, etc. words of feminine gender are formed.[4] 

<P>When Sanskrit words of feminine gender and names places, (both) ending in <i>‡,</i> are introduced, <i>‡</i> becomes <i>ai;</i> words ending in <i>„</i> end in <i>i.</i> (5) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_57">Vi_57</A>
& <A NAME="_Vi_trsl_58">Vi_58</A>
<BR>((The usage of Sanskrit words in Tamil))</H3>

<P>57 & 58. The fourfold sounds of <i>ka, ca, Òa, ta</i> and <i>pa</i> in Sanskrit are represented in Tamil by the only pentad of <i>ka, ca, Òa, ta</i> and <i>pa</i>. 

<P>When a word beginning with <i>ya</i> is Tamilised, the letter <i>ya</i> is preceded by <i>i</i>. Similarly <i>la</i> is preceded either by <i>i</i> or <i>u</i> and <i>ra</i> by <i>a, i</i> or <i>u</i>. 

<P><i>k˘a</i> is changed to  <i>k ka</i> in Tamil. 

<P>When <i>˘</i> is succeeded by <i>ka, ˘</i> also becomes <i>ka</i>. If <i>˘</i> comes in the middle of a word it becomes <i>Òa</i>. 

<P>If <i>˜a</i> or <i>sa</i> comes in the beginning of a word, it becomes <i>ca. ˜a</i> in the middle of a word becomes <i>ya</i> while <i>sa</i> in the middle becomes <i>ta</i>. 

<P>In the vowel-consonant, the consonant <i>h</i> is lost in every instance; however <i>ha</i> in the middle or the end of a word becomes <i>ya</i> or <i>ka</i>. (6), (7) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_59">Vi_59</A>
<BR>((The usage of combinations of two Sanskrit-consonants in Tamil))</H3>

 

<P>59. In Sanskrit when <i>y, r</i> and <i>l</i> happen to be the succeeding consonants of <i>c, Ò</i> and <i>t, i</i> springs in between them. 

<P>If <i>v</i> is the succeeding consonant in the middle of a word preceded by <i>k, u</i> will come into being in the middle. In all these cases the preceding hard consonant gets doubled. 

<P>Certain other changes also occur. There are to be understood by the conventions of tradition. These rules apply to Telugu, Sinhalese, etc. in certain circumstances. (8) 


<HR>

<H2>The chapter on verbal roots [60-70]</H2>

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_60">Vi_60</A>
<BR>((**))</H3>

<P>60. The verbal roots should resemble the imperative, singular finite word. (1) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_61">Vi_61</A>
<BR>((Examples of verbal roots))</H3>

 

<P>61. The examples of verbal roots, transitive or intransitive, are <i>aÒu, naÒa</i>, etc. (2) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_62">Vi_62</A>
<BR>((Rules for combining terminations with verbal roots to form verbal nouns succeeded by verbs))</H3>


<P>62. The terminations <i>v‡≠, u</i>, etc. should join the verbal roots to form verbal nouns and also nouns succeeded by verbs. (3) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_63">Vi_63</A>
<BR>((Verbal roots joining the terminations in Sanskrit))</H3>

 

<P>63. When the terminations <i>tam, am, ti</i>, etc. join verbal roots in Sanskrit they form verbal nouns and other nouns. (4) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_64">Vi_64</A>
<BR>((The formation of verbal nouns and nouns from verbal roots in Sanskrit))</H3>

<P>64. In Sanskrit the ending will be <i>ay</i> or <i>‡p</i> when verbal roots ending in <i>i</i> join terminations beginning with a vowel. The verbal roots ending in <i>u</i> become <i>av</i> or <i>‡v</i>. The roots may change according to the rules of <i>guıam</i> and <i>viratthi</i>. (5) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_65">Vi_65</A>
<BR>((Causal imperative roots))</H3>

<P>65. <i>‡ÒÒu, ‡üüu</i> are causal imperative roots. <i>‡ku, ‡üu</i> are simple roots. When the termination <i>vi</i> or <i>pi</i> succeeds the causal imperative, it becomes a double causal imperative (e. g. <i>‡ÒÒuvi</i>). If <i>pi</i> succeeds the double causal imperative the root of a triple causal imperative is formed (e. g. <i>‡ÒÒuvippi</i>). (6) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_66">Vi_66</A>
<BR>((Tense particles in adjectives))</H3>

 

<P>66.  The tense particles <i>t, ≠</i> and <i>y</i> after the verb-root express past tense. The tense particles <i>kiüa, cu</i> and <i>‡ni≠üa</i> after the verb-root express present there. The tense particles <i>kum, um</i> and <i>m</i> after the verb-root express future tense. Other tense particles also occasionally may be used. (7) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_67">Vi_67</A>
<BR>((Termination at the end of adverbs))</H3>

 

<P>67. The terminations which are <i>poruÒÒu, ka, p‡≠, taüku, v‡≠</i> and <i>a</i> when combined with the verbal roots express the present and future tenses. (8) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_68">Vi_68</A>
<BR>((Termination at the end of adverbs in the past tense))</H3>


<P>68. When an adverb modifying a finite word is formed by the combination of one of the terminations that are <i>‡, iÒÒu, tu, u</i> and <i>i</i> with the verb-root, it expresses the past tense. (9) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_69">Vi_69</A>
<BR>((The syntactical link of certain particles))</H3>

 

<P>69. There are a number of particles such as <i>illai, uıÒu, ‡l</i> and <i>il</i> which should be employed in strict adherence to the traditional rule. (10) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_70">Vi_70</A>
<BR>((Terminations used to denote negative sense))</H3>


<P>70. There are terminations, namely <i>‡≠, ‡Î, ‡r, ‡rkaÎ, ‡tu, ‡, iva≠, ivaÎ</i>, etc., which join the verbal root to express the negative sense. Certain other negative terminations are employed to form negative adjectives and adverbs. Here too, tradition is the sole guide. (11) 

 

<HR>
<H2>The chapter on finite verbs [71-85]</H2>



<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_71">Vi_71</A>
<BR>((The finite verbs in the Third Person</H3>


<P>71. The Third Person employs six kinds of finite verbs. They are the Personal masculine singular, Personal feminine singular, Personal honorific singular, Personal plural, neuter singular and neuter plural. As the tenses are three they become 18 in number. (1) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_72">Vi_72</A>
<BR>((The finite verbs in Second and First Persons))</H3>

 

<P>72. The finite verbs in the Second and First Persons which are the singular, the honorific singular and the plural become (2x3x3) 18 in number as they are used in connection with the three tenses. When these 18 are added to the 18 of the third Person, the total comes to 36. (2) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_73">Vi_73</A>
<BR>((The terminations of the verbs of the Third Person in the past tense))</H3>

 

<P>73. The terminations of the verbs of the Third Person in the past tense are twelve in number, namely <i>t‡≠, ‡≠, t‡Î, ‡Î</i>, etc. (3) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_74">Vi_74</A>
<BR>((The terminations of the verbs of the Third Person in the present tense))</H3>

 

<P>74. They are twelve in number which are <i>ni≠ü‡≠, kiü‡≠</i>, etc. (4) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_75">Vi_75</A>
<BR>((The terminations of the verbs of the Third Person in the future tense))</H3>

 

<P>75. They are twelve in number which are <i>v‡≠, p‡≠</i>, etc. (5) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_76">Vi_76</A>
<BR>((The terminations of the Second Person and First Person in the past tense))</H3>

 

<P>76. The terminations of the Second Person in the past tense are six which are <i>t‡y, ‡y</i>, etc. 

<P>The terminations of the First Persons in the past tense are six which are <i>tõ≠, õ≠</i>, etc. (6) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_77">Vi_77</A>
<BR>((The terminations of the Second Person and First Person in the present tense))</H3>

 

<P>77. The terminations of the Second Person in the present tense are six which are <i>kiü‡y, ni≠ü‡y</i>, etc. 

<P>The terminations of the First Person in the present tense are six which are <i>kiüõ≠, ni≠üe≠</i>. etc. (7) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_78">Vi_78</A>
<BR>((The terminations of the Second and First Persons in the future tense))</H3>

 

<P>78. The terminations of the Second Person in the future tense of six which are <i>v‡y, p‡y</i>, etc. (8) 

<P>The terminations of the First Person in the future tense are six which are <i>võ≠, põ≠</i>, etc. (9) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_79">Vi_79</A>
<BR>((The terminations of the imperative verbs))</H3>

 

<P>79. The verb roots in the imperative singular combine with the termination <i>cu,</i> though <i>cu</i> gets lost in the process. 

<P>The terminations of the imperative honorific singular are <i>‡mõ, um, mi≠</i> and <i>ka</i>. In these cases <i>n‡m</i> will be used in the place of <i>nIr</i>.

<P>In the imperative plural <i>mi≠kaÎ</i> and <i>umikaÎ</i> are the terminations. 

<P>The termination <i>ka</i> is introduced to ascertain consent. 

<P>When there is no certainty, the terminations <i>pùlum</i> and <i>‡</i> will follow mostly the verbs of all tenses. (9) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_80">Vi_80</A>
<BR>((The terminations of verbs denoting certainty))</H3>

 

<P>80. In First Person singular <i>va≠</i> and <i>pa≠</i> follow the verb roots. 

<P>In First Person plural the 4 terminations are <i>tam, cam, pùm</i> and <i>vùm</i>. 

<P>In Third Person singular <i>um</i> is the termination. 

<P>In third Person plural <i>var, par, varkaÎ</i> and <i>parkaÎ</i> are termination.

<P>In second Person singular the three terminations, <i>ci, vai</i> and <i>ti</i> follow the verb roots. 

<P>In second Person plural <i>kir, k„r, vir, v„r, cir, c„r, tir, t„r, pir</i> and <i>p„r</i> are the terminations. 

<P>All these denote certainty. (10) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_81">Vi_81</A>
<BR>((The form of verbal noun; the form of object becoming the subject; the finite verb becoming a noun and performing its (noun's) action))</H3>

 

<P>81. The verbal noun in Tamil assumes the form of Third Person neuter singular verb. 

<P>When the object becomes the subject, the termination <i>paÒu</i> joins the verb-root form and the necessary terminations occur. 

<P>When the finite verb becomes a noun, it has its syntactical link with the verbs in the nominative case and assumes the usual case-endings in the other cases. (11) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_82">Vi_82</A>
<BR>((Flawless pronunciation and writing))</H3>



<P>82. <i>©a, Îa</i> and <i>üa, ca</i> are not exchangeable. 

<P>Common people commit a number of errors/mistakes/blander. They are to be eschewed by a proper study of the writing and speeches of the scholars. (12) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_83">Vi_83</A>
<BR>((Avoidance of mistakes by understanding the traditions of Sanskrit and Tamil))</H3>

 

<P>83. One should avoid the commission of mistakes, etc. by a proper study of the specialities of Sanskrit and Tamil. (13) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_84">Vi_84</A>
<BR>((Analysis of the Chapter on words))</H3>

 

<P>84. The chapter on words explicates the eight case-ending the personal and the impersonal, the five genders, the seven kinds of errors, the six kinds of ellipsis, the three terms, the three persons and the two traditions relating to prosody and usage. (14) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_85">Vi_85</A>
<BR>((Avinaya≠‡r on words.))</H3>

 

<P>85. <i>tiıai</i> (the Personal and Impersonal), <i>p‡l</i> (gender), <i>marapu</i> (tradition), <i>vi≠‡</i> (interrogation), <i>viÒai</i> (answer), <i>iÒam</i> (the three persons), <i>col</i> (kinds of words), <i>va©u</i> (errors, etc.), <i>tokai</i> (ellipsis), <i>eccam</i> (omissions/adjectives/adverbs), <i>võüüumai</i> (case-endings), <i>k‡lam</i> (the three tenses), <i>k‡lamayakku</i> (the change in the tenses), etc. have all been explained by Avinaya≠‡r. (15) 

 
<HR>

<H1>The Section on PoruÎ</H1>

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_86">Vi_86</A>
<BR>((Four classifications))</H3>

 

<P>86. PoruÎ which is subject-matter is classified into four, namely, Akam, Puüam, Aka-p-puüam and Puüa-p-puüam. It may also be classified into three 1) means of acquiring correct knowledge of a thing 2) one who applies the same and 3) the object which is subjected to that means, being called in Sanskrit respectively <i>pram‡≠am</i>, <i>pram‡ta</i> and <i>pramõyam</i>. (1) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_87">Vi_87</A>
& <A NAME="_Vi_trsl_88">Vi_88</A>
<BR>((How they are subdivided))</H3>

 

<P>87&88. Of the above four classifications, Akam which is love-theme is subdivided into five thematic settings called <i>tiıai</i>, namely Mullai, Kuüi§ci, Marutam, Neytal and P‡lai, Puüam which comprises mostly war-operations and heroic eulogies into seven, namely, VeÒci, Karantai, Va§c,i  K‡§ci, Nocci, U©i§ai and Tumpai, Aka-p-puüam into twelve, namely, KaikkiÎai, Peruntiıai, K‡ntaÎ, Mutupalai, Cura-maÒai, IllavaÎ-mullai, Taput‡ram, T‡patam, VaÎÎi, KuüuÔkali, Kuüüicai and P‡caüai-mullai and Puüa-p-puüam into three, namely, V‡kai, P‡Ò‡≠, and Potu-viyal. (2), (3) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_89">Vi_89</A>
<BR><BR>Twenty seven requisites))</H3>

 

<P>89. For the above five subdivisions of Akam such as Mullai, there are twenty seven requisites of commentary which are <i>caÒÒakam, tiıai</i>, etc. They take place with reference to Puüam, Akappuüam and Puüappuüam also befittingly. 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_90">Vi_90</A>
& <A NAME="_Vi_trsl_91">Vi_91</A>
<BR>((**))</H3>

<P>90&91. The above 27 requisites are
<UL>
<LI>1. <i>caÒÒakam</i> 
<LI>2. <i>tiıai</i> 
<LI>3. <i>kaikùÎ</i> 
<LI>4. <i>naÒai</i> 
<LI>5. <i>cuÒÒu</i> 
<LI>6. <i>iÒa≠</i> 
<LI>7. <i>kiÎavi</i> 
<LI>8. <i>kõÎvi</i>  
<LI>9. <i>mo©i-vakai</i> 
<LI>10. <i>kùÎ</i> 
<LI>11. <i>uÒpeüu-poruÎ</i> 
<LI>12. <i>coüporuÎ 
<LI></i>13. <i>eccam</i> 
<LI>14.<i> iüaicci</i> 
<LI>15. <i>paya≠</i> 
<LI>16. <i>kuüippu</i> 
<LI>17. <i>meypp‡Òu</i> 
<LI>18. <i>k‡ra≠am</i> 
<LI>19. <i>k‡lam</i> 
<LI>20. <i>karuttu</i> 
<LI>21. <i>iyalpu</i> 
<LI>22. <i>viÎaivu</i> 
<LI>23. <i>uvamam</i> 
<LI>24. <i>ilakkaıam</i> 
<LI>25. <i>puÒai-y-urai</i> 
<LI>26. <i>mo©i-cõr-ta≠mai</i> 
<LI>and 27. <i>poruÎ-aÒaivu</i>.
</UL>

 
<UL>
<LI>1. Poetical form in a particular metre,
<LI>2. Thematic setting such as Kuüi§ci,
<LI>3. Clandestine love and marital,
<LI>4. Conduct belonging to a particular setting (<i>tiıai</i>), 
<LI>5. Referring to I person, II and III,
<LI>6. Place, whether it be within the proximity of the house or without,
<LI>7. Speaker,
<LI>8. Listener,
<LI>9. Part of speech whether the words are natural, variable, provincial or Sanskrit derivatives,
<LI>10. Mode of construction (<i>poruÎ-kùÎ</i>),
<LI>11. Inner significance,
<LI>12. Word-by-word meaning, paraphrase, etc.,
<LI>13. Adjectives and adverbs (and also other elliptical forms),
<LI>14. Distinctive regional features,
<LI>15. Goal or effect,
<LI>16. Expression through outward action concealing one's thoughts,
<LI>17. Physical expressions of one's mental emotions,
<LI>18. Reason,
<LI>19. Threefold time,
<LI>20. Pith of a matter,
<LI>21. Saying that a certain action is met within a certain place,
<LI>22. Result as per one's desire,
<LI>23. Comparison,
<LI>24. Defining certain terms,
<LI>25. What remaining still to be understood besides the actual signification,
<LI>26. Compound words formed by means of ellipses,
<LI>27. Orderly placement of things spoken.
</UL>
 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_92">Vi_92</A>,
<A NAME="_Vi_trsl_93">Vi_93</A>,
<A NAME="_Vi_trsl_94">Vi_94</A>,
<A NAME="_Vi_trsl_95">Vi_95</A>
& <A NAME="_Vi_trsl_96">Vi_96</A>
<BR><i>Mutal-poruÎ, karu-p-poruÎ</i> and <i>uri-p-poruÎ</i> of the above five <i>tiıais</i>))</H3>

 

<P>92-96. There <i>k‡rikai</i> verses enlist the details of all the five thematic settings, namely, Kuüi§ci, P‡lai, Mullai, Marutam and Neytal, pointing out a) region b) long and short periods (these two belong to <i>mutal-poruÎ</i>, vital object), c) distinctive regional features (<i>karu-p-poruÎ</i>) and erotic mood (<i>uri-p-poruÎ</i>) respectively. Here the distinctive regional features pertain to the deity of the region, common people, vegetations, birds, flowers professions of the inhabitants, food, residing hamlet, etc., water-sources and animals. 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_97">Vi_97</A>
<BR>Twelve sub-divisions of Aka-p-puüam))</H3>

 

<P>97. They are, as enumerated in this <i>k‡rikai</i>, Mutu-p‡lai, P‡caüai-mullai, VaÎÎi, CuranaÒai, IllavaÎ-mullai, K‡ntaÎ, KaüuÔkali, T‡patam, Kuüüicai, KaikkiÎai, Perun-tiıai and Tapu-t‡ram. 

 
<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_98">Vi_98</A>
<BR>((Puüam and Puüa-p-puüam))</H3>

 
<P>98. In this <i>k‡rikai</i> all the seven thematic settings of Puram and three of Puüa-p-puüam are mentioned. 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_99">Vi_99</A>,
<A NAME="_Vi_trsl_100">Vi_100</A>,
<A NAME="_Vi_trsl_101">Vi_101</A>,
<A NAME="_Vi_trsl_102">Vi_102</A>,
<A NAME="_Vi_trsl_103">Vi_103</A>,
<A NAME="_Vi_trsl_104">Vi_104</A>,
<A NAME="_Vi_trsl_105">Vi_105</A>,
& <A NAME="_Vi_trsl_106">Vi_106</A>
<BR>((Themes of VeÒci, Karantai, etc.))</H3>

 

<P>99-106. Classified themes of VeÒci, Karantai, Va§ci, K‡§ci, U©i§ai, Tumpai, V‡kai and P‡Ò‡≠ which are respectively 14, 9, 11, 12, 10, 5, 14 and 6 in number are enumerated, following partially the Puüattiıai-y-yal of Tolk‡ppiyam. The definitions of these themes are found only in the commentary. 

 

<HR>
<H1>The Section on Prosody</H1>

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_107">Vi_107</A>
& <A NAME="_Vi_trsl_108">Vi_108</A>
<BR>((Twofold metreme and twofold metrical foot))</H3>

 

<P>107&108. A short letter, the same followed by a consonant, a long vowel and the same followed by a consonant --- all there four are called <i>nõr</i> metreme. Two short letters, the same followed by a consonant a short letter and a long letter and the same followed by a consonant --- all these four are called <i>nirai</i> metreme. A metrical foot of two metremes is termed <i>mutaü-c„r</i> and these of three metreme ending with <i>nõr</i> and <i>nirai</i> respectively are termed <i>iÒai-c-c„r</i> and <i>kaÒai-c-c„r</i>. 

<P><i>Karu-viÎam, kÂ-viÎam, tõ-m‡</i> and <i>puÎi-m‡</i> are symbolic expressions of <i>mutaü-c„r</i>. If they end with <i>k‡y</i> and <i>ka≠i</i>, they are respectively symbolic expressions of <i>iÒai-c-c„r</i> and <i>kaÒai-c-c„r</i>. (1), (2) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_109">Vi_109</A>
<BR>((Fourfold metrical line))</H3>


<P>109. Lines made up of 2 metrical feet, 3, 4, 5 and 6 and above are called respectively the sky, the air, the fire, the water and the zenith. They are also named, by some prosodist, <i>kuüaÎ, cintu, aÎava, meÒil</i> and <i>kaÎi-meÒil</i> respectively which are dwarf line, short line, standard line, long line and overlong line. (3) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_110">Vi_110</A>
<BR>((Alliterative letters))</H3>

 

<P>110.
<UL>
<LI>In vowels,
<UL>
<LI><i>a, ‡, ai, au</i> --- all these 4 alliterate with one another.
<LI><i>i, „, e, õ</i> --- all these 4 alliterate with one another
<LI><i>u, Â, o, ù</i> --- all these 4 alliterate with one another
</UL>
<LI>In consonants, 
<UL>
<LI><i>t</i> and <i>c</i> alliterate with each other.
<LI><i>n</i> and § "  "  
<LI><i>m</i> and <i>v</i> "  "
</UL>
<LI>(This holds good in the case of vowel-consonant also.)  (4) 
</UL>
 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_111">Vi_111</A>
<BR>((Rhyme and its kinds))</H3>

 

<P>111.     In each line of a verse, if the second letters of the first feet are the same, the first letters being of the same unit of sound (<i>m‡ttirai</i>), rhyme occurs there. It is of various kinds, namely
<UL>
<LI>a) rhyme where in the metrical feet the third letters being the same,
<LI>b) rhyme taking place in the first feet of every two lines, 
<LI>c) rhyme where the entire feet excepting the first letters are the same, 
<LI>d) rhyme where the second letters of the rhyming feet are consonants which though not the same belong to the kindred of sounds, that of nasal and that of liquids, 
<LI>e) rhyme where in the rhyming feet the second letters are the same consonant though animated by different vowels. 
<LI>f) rhyme where in the rhyming feet the second letters are the same vowel though animating different consonants, and 
<LI>g) rhyme where the consonants <i>y r l</i> or <i>©</i> intervene between the first long letter and the second metreme without hindrance (thereto). (5) 
</UL>
 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_111">Vi_111</A>
<BR>((Pattiyam and kattiyam ))</H3>



<P>112. Poetical composition is of two kinds, namely, <i>pattiyam</i> and <i>kattiyam</i>. <i>Padyam</i> takes place by means of <i>p‡dam</i> --- metrical lines. <i>Kadyam</i> is of two kinds which are <i>kaÒÒurai-pùli</i> and <i>ceyyuÒ-pùli</i> (those that resemble prose and those that resemble poetry). (6) 



<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_113">Vi_113</A>
& <A NAME="_Vi_trsl_114">Vi_114</A>
<BR>((Ve≠p‡ and its kinds))</H3>

<P>113 & 114. In Ve≠p‡ only <i>mutaü-c„r</i> and <i>iÒai-c-c„r</i> take place. The last metreme of the former will be agreeable to the first metreme of the succeeding metrical foot while that of the latter won't be so. The last line of Ve≠p‡ is of three feet and all the other three lines are of four feet. The last metrical foot of the last line ends as either single syllable --- <i>nõr</i> and <i>nirai</i> or as <i>nõr</i>, two syllables --- <i>nõr-nõr</i> and <i>nirai-nõr</i> with <i>u</i>-ending, where symbolic expressions of the four are respectively <i>n‡Î, malar, k‡cu</i> and <i>piüappam</i>. 

<P>The Ve≠p‡ occurring in 2 lines is called KuüaÎ-ve≠p‡, that occurring in 3 lines Cintiyal-ve≠p‡, that occurring in 4 lines Nõricai-ve≠p‡, that occurring in more than 4 lines PakuüoÒai-ve≠p‡, that occurring as Nõricai-ve≠p‡ having more lines Kali-ve≠p‡ that occurring in 4 lines contrary to Nõricai-ve≠p‡, I≠≠icai-ve≠p‡ and (7), (8) 



<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_115">Vi_115</A>
<BR>((Kinds of ‚ciriyam))</H3>


<P>115. The metre of ‚ciriyam in which the penultimate line also occurs with three metrical feet while all the other lines occur with four metrical feet is called Nõricai-‡ciriyam, that where the first line and the last are of four feet while the mid-lines have two or three (or five) metrical feet is called I≠aikkuüaÎ-‡ciriyam, that where all the lines occur with four metrical feet is called Vilai-ma≠Òilam and that where every line has a complete sense and any line may be transposed in the beginning, in the middle or at the end as per one's will, without injury to the metre or sense is called AÒi-m‡üi- ma≠Òila-‡ciriyapp‡. (9) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_116">Vi_116</A>
<BR>((Kinds of Kali))</H3>


<P>116. Kali-metre has all the six constituent members, namely Taravu, T‡©icai (which comprises 3 stanzas), Ar‡kam, AmpùtaraÔkam detached word and Curitakam. It is of various kinds called Nõricai-ott‡©icai-k-kalipp‡, AmpùtaraÔka-otta©icai-k-kalipp‡, Va≠≠aka-otta©icai-k-kalipp‡, Koccaka-k-kalipp‡ and Veı-kalipp‡. (10) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_117">Vi_117</A>
<BR>((Occurrence of Va≠≠aka-ott‡©icai-k-kalipp‡, etc.))</H3>


<P>117.  Va≠≠aka-ott‡©icai-k-kalipp‡ occurs with all the six constituent members such as Taravu and T‡©icai; AmpùtaraÔka-ott‡©icai-k-kalipp‡ occurs with the remaining five members excepting Ar‡kam; barring Ar‡kam and AmpùtaraÔkam with the remaining four members takes place Nõricai-ott‡Îicai-k-kalipp‡. Veı-kalipp‡ ends with a three foot line while all other lines are of four metrical feet each. (11) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_118">Vi_118</A>
<BR>((Kinds of Koccaka-k-kali))</H3>


<P>118.  Koccaka-k-kalipp‡ is classified into five various kinds namely, Koccaka-k-kalipp‡ occurring with one <i>Taravu</i>, Koccaka-k-kalipp‡ occurring with a pair of Taravu Koccaka-k-kalipp‡ occurring with a few number of T‡©icai verses, Koccaka-k-kalipp‡ occurring with a number of T‡licai verses and Koccaka-k-kalipp‡ in which all the constituent members occur irregularly and mixed, deviating from their rules with less or more metrical quantities than required. (12) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_119">Vi_119</A>
<BR>((The metres of Va§ci and MaruÒip‡))</H3>

 

<P>119. Va§ci metre occurs with lines of 2 feet or 3 feet in the beginning (which are Va§ci lines) and ends with Curitakam-‡ciriyam lines, prececed by a detached word. ManuÒ-p‡ is a verse which begins with Venp‡ lines and ends with ‚ciriyam lines. (13) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_120">Vi_120</A>
<BR>((The occurrence of <i>kÂ≠</i>; Ve≠-centuüai and KuüaÒÒ‡©icai))</H3>

 

<P>120. <i>KÂ≠</i> is a word which stands at the beginning of a metrical line, with sense. It may even occur in the middle and end of a line of Va§ci metre. Ve≠-centuüai occurs in 2 lines each of equal metrical quantity which is mostly 4 metrical feet. Ve≠-centuüai having no sublime theme and flowing rhythm, KuüaÎ-ve≠p‡ deviating from its Ceppal rhythm and a verse of lines with a number of metrical feet, the second line having less number of feet --- all these three types are termed KuüaÎ-t‡©icai. (14) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_121">Vi_121</A>
<BR>((VeÎi-viruttam, Ve≠Ò‡©icai and Ve≠Òuüai))</H3>

 

<P>121. VeÎi-viruttam occurs in 3 or 4 lines having the same detached metrical foot at the end of every line. If Cintiyal-ve≠p‡ gets deviated from its rhythm of Ceppal, it becomes Ve≠Ò‡©icai. It is Ve≠Òuüai when Nõricai-ve≠p‡ fails in its Ceppal rhythm. Ve≠Òuüai has also lines raging between 3 and 7 having less number of metrical feet in some lines of the latter half. (15) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_122">Vi_122</A>
<BR>((‚ciriya-t-t‡©icai, ‚ciriya-t-tuüai and ‚ciriya-viruttam))</H3>

 

<P>122. A verse having 3 lines with equal metrical quantity is called ‚ciriya-t-t‡©icai which may occur either singly or as three stanzas on one theme. ‚ciriya-t-tuüai occurs as verse having 4 lines where the penultimate is deficient (by one foot), where the second line repeats as the third and where the lines are deficient alternately. There are occasions where the mid-lines have two more metrical feet each and two less metrical feet each, where the first line has less number of feet and where the lines are overlong (having 6 and more metrical feet). ‚ciriya-viruttam is a verse which has 4 lines each having 6 metrical feet or more. (16.) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_123">Vi_123</A>
<BR>((Kali-t-t‡©icai, Kali-viruttam and Kali-t-tuüai))</H3>


<P>123. Kali-t-t‡©icai is a verse having 2 or more lines in which the last line sounds longer with more number of metrical feet. This may even occur as 3 verses on one theme.   Kali-viruttam is a verse of 4 lines having 4 metrical feet each. Kali-t-tuüai occurs with 4 lines each of 5 metrical feet. (17) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_124">Vi_124</A>
<BR>((Kùvai-k-kali-t-tuüai))</H3>

 

<P>124. Kùvai-k-kali-t-tuüai is a verse of 4 lines each of five metrical feet. If a line begins with <i>nõr</i>, it has 16 letters and if <i>nirai</i> is its beginning metreme it has 17 letters. (The first 4 metrical feet should not deviate from Ve≠ÒaÎai. While reckoning the letters, consonants and the guttural are ignored. (18) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_125">Vi_125</A>
<BR>((Va§ci-t-tuüai, Va§ci-viruttan and Va§ci-t-t‡©icai))</H3>


<P>125.     A verse occurring with two-foot lines is called Va§ci-t-ta. Va§ci-viruttam is a verse of lines each having three metrical feet. It is named Va§ci-t-t‡©icai, if Va§ci-t-tuüai occurs as three stanzas on one theme. 

<P>Other verses also may be included in all these three various verses --- T‡©icai, Tuüai and Viruttam considering their similar aspects (alone). (19) 

<P>So far the author has dealt with the opinion of other prosodists with regard to classifications of various metres. His own view is expatiated upon in the following <i>k‡rikais</i>. This is as per the view of the commentator, PerunÒõva≠‡r. 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_126">Vi_126</A>
<BR>((Sevenfold Pattiyam))</H3>


<P>126.  Pattiyam --- poetical composition --- is sevenfold, namely KuüaÎ, Cintu, Tirip‡ti, Ve≠p‡, Tilatam, Virattam and Cavalai. If these seven kinds deviate from their rules and occur with more or less metrical quantities they are termed similitudes of the corresponding verse which are KuüaÒ-pùti, Cintu-p-pùli, Tirip‡t„-p-pùli, Ve≠p‡-p-pùli, Tilata-p-pùli, Viratta pùli and Cavalai-p-pùli. (20) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_127">Vi_127</A>
<BR>((The first four defined))</H3>


<P>127.  KuüaÎ has 7 metrical feet in two lines. Cintu occurs in two lines each having equal number (mostly four) of metrical feet. Tirip‡ti is met with in three lines of equal metrical quantity each. Ve≠p‡ takes place in four lines comprising 15 metrical feet in all, with one detached foot at the end of the second line (rhyming with first feet of the first two lines). (21) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_128">Vi_128</A>
<BR>((Tilatam defined))</H3>


<P>128.  Tilatam is a four-line verse, each line comprising five metrical feet. The line beginning with <i>nõr</i> has 16 letters while that beginning with <i>nirai</i> has 17. [The subtle difference lies between Kùvai-k-kali-t-taüai and Tilata-k-kali-t-tuüai (as per some antique foot-rule notes on the old commentary) in the formation of the fourth line where the third foot in Kùvai-k-kali-t-tuüai occurs in disregard to speech rhythm while it is not so in the case of Tilata-k-kali-totuüai. e.g. poem "<i>kaÒikama© kùtai nal l‡y paııu kùvaik kalittuüaiyõ</i>" --- as per Kùvai. "<i>kaÒikama© kùtaik kaya≠eÒuÔ kaııi kalittuüaiyõ</i>" --- as per Tilatam. (22) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_129">Vi_129</A>
<BR>((Viruttam defined))</H3>


<P>129.     A verse with four lines each comprising four metrical feet is called Viruttam. (23) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_130">Vi_130</A>
<BR>((Cavalai and Cavalai-p-pùli))</H3>


<P>130. Cavalai is a four-line verse where the first line, the last line or the middle lines have either more metrical fees or less (than those in other lines). If the first line is deficient it is called Mutaü-cavalai. It is termed IÒai-c-cavalai and KaÒai-c-cavalai respectively if the mid-line or the last line of the verse is so deficient. Cavalai-p-pùli is a verse which occurs in more than four lines which are of the same metrical quantity or unlike with varying number of feet in every line. (24) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_131">Vi_131</A>
<BR>((Sanskrit Viruttam and TaıÒakam))</H3>


<P>131. Some prosodists say that Viruttam occurs even from one letter per line (up to the maximum of 26). Unless a line comprises the mimimum of 4 letters, rhythm won't be apparent. The maximum number of letters for a line of Viruttam is 26. Those that occur with more than 26 letters per line are termed Virutta-p-pùli (similitude of Viruttam). Ta≠Òakam occurs with a minimum of four KattaÎais in the verse. (25) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_132">Vi_132</A>
<BR>((<i>Kuru</i> and <i>ilaku</i> defined.))</H3>


<P>132. A single long letter, the same followed by a consonant and a short letter followed by a consonant --- all these three are termed <i>kuru</i> which has 2 <i>alakus</i> and bears a symbol resembling a crescent moon [{{VERIF.MS}}]. A single short letter is <i>ilaku</i> which has one <i>alaku</i> and bears a symbol of a perpendicular line drawn downwards [|{{VERIF.MS}}]. The <i>ilaku</i> at the end of a line probably becomes a <i>kuru</i>. (26) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_133">Vi_133</A>
<BR>((Clarity being six-fold))</H3>


<P>133. The six clarities are 1) <i>uüa©cci</i>, 2) <i>naÒÒam</i>, 3) <i>uttiÒÒam</i>, 4) <i>ilaku-kuru</i> operation which calculates as to how many lines have one <i>ilaku</i>, two <i>ilakus</i>, one <i>kuru</i>, two <i>kurus</i> and so on, 5) ascertaining total number of Viruttam as to how many belong to the first <i>cantam</i>, second <i>cantam</i> and so on up to the 26th <i>cantam</i> and finally 6) area of land occupying space to write all the <i>cantams</i> with all its individual kinds. (27) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_134">Vi_134</A>
<BR>((Uüa©cci (which is <i>piratt‡ram)</i> defined))</H3>


<P>134. Enumeration of all possible combinations of metrical syllables (which are <i>kuru</i> and <i>ilaku</i>) in a given verse-mode are Uüa©cci. Take a four lettered line having complete <i>kuru</i> (k) and alter the positions of <i>kuru</i> and <i>ilaku</i> as shown hereunder. Likewise take a four-lettered line having complete <i>ilaku</i> (L) and alter the positions of <i>kuru</i> and <i>ilaku</i> as depicted below.


<P>I II 


<P> K K K K L L L L 


<P> L K K K K L L L 


<P> K L K K L K L L 


<P> L L K K K K L L 


<P> K K L K L L K- L 


<P> L K L K K L K L 


<P> K L L K L K K L 


<P> L L L K K K K L 


<P>So much so we get 16 four lettered <i>cantam</i> out of which 8 end in <i>kuru</i> and 8 in <i>ilaku</i>. In this way 5 lettered <i>cantam</i> is 32 out of which 16 end in <i>kuru</i> and 16 in <i>ilaku</i>. 

<P>6 lettered Cantam is 64, 7 lettered in 128, 8 lettered is 256 and so on. 


<P> I II 


<P> K K K K K L L L L L 


<P> L K K K K K L L L L 


<P> K L K K K L K L L L 


<P> L L K K K K K L L L 


<P> K K L K K L L K L L 


<P> L K L K K K L K L L 


<P> K L L K K L K K L L 


<P> L L L K K K K K L L 


<P> K K K L K L L L K L 


<P> L K K L K K L L K L 


<P> K L K L K L K L K L 


<P> L L K L K K K L K L 


<P> K K L L K L L K K L 


<P> L K L L K K L K K L 


<P> K L L L K L K K K L 


<P> L L L L K K K K K L (28) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_135">Vi_135</A>
<BR>((NaÒÒam defined))</H3>


<P>135.  NaÒÒam is the method of finding out letters (of the line) of a particular <i>cantam</i> standing in a particular order as per <i>piratt‡ram</i>. e.g. 

<UL>
<LI>(a) Find out the line of five-letter <i>cantam</i> ending in <i>kuru</i> standing in the 9th order. 

<LI>(b) Find out the line of five-letter <i>cantam</i> ending in <i>ilaku</i> standing in the 12th order. (a) No. 9 is odd. This is <i>kuru</i>. As 9 cannot be divided equally add one unto 9. The quotient is [(9+1)/2; 10/2] 5 which is also odd. This is also <i>kuru</i>.
</UL>
 

<P>The same process follows:  
<UL>
<LI>[(5+1)/2; 6/2] 3 is also odd. This is also <i>kuru</i>. 
<LI>[(3+1)/2; 4/2] 2 is even. This is <i>ilaku</i>. 
<LI>[2/2] 1 is odd.  This is <i>kuru</i>. 
</UL>

<P>Therefore the 9th five-letter <i>cantam</i> is K K K L K In(a) if the dividend is odd mark <i>kuru</i>; if it is even mark <i>ilaku</i>. This process is to be opposite with regard to <i>ilaku</i>-ending <i>cantam</i> (l), That is, if the dividend is odd mark <i>ilaku</i>; if it is even mark <i>kuru</i>. 

<UL>
<LI>No. 12 is even and so marks <i>kuru</i>. 
<LI>12/2=6 which is also even and so mark <i>kuru</i>. 
<LI>6/2=3 which is odd and so mark <i>ilaku</i>. 
<LI>(3+1)/2 = 2 which is even and so mark <i>kuru</i>. 
<LI>2/2=1 which is odd and so mark <i>ilaku</i>. 
</UL>

<P>Therefore the 12th five-letter Cantam is K K L K L. (29) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_136">Vi_136</A>
<BR>((UttiÒÒam defined))</H3>

<P>136. This clarity of UttiÒÒam consists in finding out the order of place where a particular Cantam (of particular number of letters) stands as per <i>piratt‡ram</i>. The method of calculating the same is enunciated in this <i>k‡rikai</i>. e.g.
<UL>
<LI>a) Find out the order of place of the five-letter Cantam L L K L K.
<LI>b) Find out the same of the five letter Cantam, L K L K L. 
</UL>

<UL>
<LI>a) On every letter put number beginning from 1 orderly, each being doubled till the last. For <i>kuru</i>-ending Cantam total the numbers prescribed an <i>ilaku</i> and then add one. The proceed No. denote the order of placement of that Cantam.
<UL>
<LI>1 2 4 8 16 
<LI>L L K L K; 1+]
<LI>This Cantam stands in the 12th place.
</UL>

<LI>b) The same process should be repeated.
</UL>

<P>Now for <i>ilaku</i>-ending Cantam, total the numbers scribed on <i>kuru</i> and then add one. The proceed No. denotes the order of placement of that <i>ilaku</i>-ending Cantam.

<DL>
<DT>1 2 4 8 16
<DD>2+8=10
<DT>L K L K L; 2+]
<DD>10-1=11
</DL>

<P>This stands in the 11th order of the five-letter Cantam ending in <i>ilaku</i> or in the (16+11) 27th order of the five-letter Cantam which in 32 in total. (30) 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_137">Vi_137</A>
<BR>((Finding out the total number of Viruttams having one <i>ilaku</i>, one <i>kuru</i>, two <i>ilakus</i>, two <i>kurus</i> and so on))</H3>

<P>137. This <i>k‡rikai</i> points out the way to discover, in a particular Cantam, as to how many Viruttams have one <i>ilaku</i>, one <i>kuru</i>, two <i>ilakus</i>, two <i>kurus</i> and so on. 

<P>This clarity is named <i>õka-dvi-‡ti-laghu-guru-kriy‡</i> in Sanskrit and <i>ilaku-kuru-c-ceykai</i> (<i>ilaku-kuru</i> operation) in Tamil. This is the mode of calculating the total number Viruttams having a single <i>ilaku</i>, two <i>ilakus</i>, a single <i>kuru</i> two <i>kurus</i> and so on up to the Cantam of 26 letters per line. 

<UL>
<LI>{^1^} all the 4 <i>kuru</i> 
<LI>{^1^} {^3^} = 4 3 <i>kuru</i> and 1 <i>ilaku</i> 
<LI>{^1^} {^2^} {^3^} = 6 2 <i>kuru</i> and 2 <i>ilaku</i> 
<LI>{^1^} {^1^} {^1^} {^1^} = 4 1 <i>kuru</i> and 3 <i>ilaku</i> 
<LI>{*} 1 all the four <i>ilaku</i> 
<LI>

<UL>
<LI>In a four-letter Cantam, per line 
<LI>one Viruttam has 4 <i>kurus</i> completes 
<LI>four Viruttams have 3 <i>kurus</i> and 1 
<LI>six Viruttams have 2 <i>kurus</i> and 2 
<LI>four Viruttams have 1 <i>kuru</i> and 
<LI>of one Viruttam has 4 <i>ilaku</i> 
<LI>completely 
<LI>{^1^} - 1 all the 5 <i>kuru</i> 
<LI>{^1^} {^4^} = 5 4 <i>kuru</i> and 1 <i>ilaku</i> 
<LI>{^1^} {^3^} {^6^} = 10 3 <i>kuru</i> and 2 <i>ilaku</i> 
<LI>{^1^} {^2^} {^3^} {^4^} = 10 2 <i>kuru</i> and 3 <i>ilaku</i> 
<LI>{^1^} {^1^} {^1^} {^1^} {^1^} = 5 1 <i>kuru</i> and 4 <i>ilaku</i> 
<LI>{*} 1 all the 5 <i>kuru</i> 
<LI>In a five-letter Cantam, per line
<LI>One Viruttam has 5 <i>kuru</i> completely 
<LI>five Viruttams have 4 <i>kuru</i> and 1L 
<LI>ten Viruttams have 3 <i>kuru</i> and 2L 
<LI>ten Viruttams have 2 <i>kuru</i> and 3L 
<LI>five Viruttams have 1 <i>kuru</i> and 4L 
<LI>of one Viruttam has 5L complete. 
<LI>{^1^} - 1 all the 6 <i>kuru</i> 
<LI>{^1^} {^5^} = 6 5 <i>kuru</i> & 1 <i>ilaku</i> 
<LI>{^1^} {^4^} {^10^} = 15 4 <i>kuru</i> and 2 <i>ilaku</i> 
<LI>{^1^} {^3^} {^6^} {^10^} = 20 3 <i>kuru</i> and 3 <i>ilaku</i> 
<LI>{^1^} {^2^} {^3^} {^4^} {^5^} = 15 2 <i>kuru</i> and 4 <i>ilaku</i> 
<LI>{^1^} {^1^} {^1^} {^1^} {^1^} = 6 1 <i>kuru</i> and 5 <i>ilaku</i>
<LI>{*} 1 - all the 6 <i>ilaku</i>
<LI>In a six-letter Cantam, for line, 
<LI>One Viruttam has 6  <i>kuru</i> completely. 
<LI>six Viruttams have 5 <i>kuru</i> and 1 <i>ilaku</i>
<LI>fifteen Viruttams have 4 <i>kuru</i> and 2 <i>ilaku</i>
<LI>twenty Viruttams have 3 <i>kuru</i> and 3 <i>ilaku</i>
<LI>fifteen Viruttams have 2 <i>kuru</i> and 4 <i>ilaku</i>
<LI>six Viruttams have 1 <i>kuru</i> and 5<i> ilaku </i>
<LI>and one Viruttam has 6 <i>ilaku</i> complete. 
</UL>

</UL>

<P>In this way <i>ilaku-kuru</i> operation may be calculated to a Cantam having any number of letters per line up to 26. 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_138">Vi_138</A>
<BR>((How to calculate the total No. of letters, <i>i©aku, kuru</i>, and units of round (<i>m‡ttirai</i>) of a particular Cantam))</H3>


<P>138. The total No. of the particular Cantam is to be written four times at four places horizontally. They are individually to be multiplied by the number of letters (of each line) of the Cantam. The central two numbers are to be divided. One half should be added to the fourth number. Now the resultant four figures denote respectively the total No. of letters of that Cantam the total No. of <i>ilaku</i>, the total No. of <i>kuru</i> and the total No. of units of sound of that Cantam in all. 

<UL>
<LI>Let us take four-letter Cantam which is 16. 
<LI>16 16 16 16 
<LI>multiplied by 4 64 64 64 64 
<LI>the middle two 
<LI>being halved  32 32  
<LI>the last one being 
<LI>added one half of 
<LI>the above    96 
<LI>Therefore the total No. of letters of that Cantam is 64 
<LI>" of <i>ilaku</i> " 32 
<LI>" of <i>kuru</i> " 32 
<LI>" of <i>m‡ttirai</i> " 96 (32) 
</UL>
 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_139">Vi_139</A>
<BR>((Total No. of particular Cantam; the space occupied when written on ground.))</H3>

 

<P>139.  A one-letter Cantam is 2, two lettered is 4, three lettered is 8, four lettered is 16, five lettered is 32, six lettered is 64 and thus for a Cantam having one more letter (per line), the product of the previous Cantam is to be doubled. In this way we can calculate the total No. of any particular Cantam. If that product is doubled and one is substracted therefrom, it is the measurement of space in terms of finger-length for writing all the verses of that particular Cantam on the ground. A letter is supposed to occupy the space of one finger-square, (that is, one letter's length and width are the length of one finger each.)  (33) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_140">Vi_140</A>
& <A NAME="_Vi_trsl_141">Vi_141</A>
<BR>((TaıÒakam defined))</H3>

<P>140&141. The five symbols are <i>nirai</i> metreme for a long letter, <i>nõr</i> metreme for a short letter, dot for a hard consonant, circle for a soft consonant and<i> vilaku</i> for a medial consonant. 

<P>With the above symbols a KattaÎai fragment (<i>tuıÒam</i>) is noted. With such <i>tuıÒam</i> numbering 4, 8, 16 and 32, a TaıÒakam is completed. There is scarcely a symbol where soft and medical consonants are not pronounced. (34), (35) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_142">Vi_142</A>
<BR>((Twenty Va≠≠ams))</H3>


<P>142. The author enumerates twenty Vaııams which are rhythmic variations as pointed out by Tolk‡ppiya≠‡r in CeyyuÎiyal. Of course the order is irregular, may be due to composition of <i>k‡rikai</i>. (36) 


<HR>
<H1>The Section on Figures</H1>

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_143">Vi_143</A>
<BR>((Topics of this chapter))</H3>


<P>143. The author DaıÒi in his treatise K‡vyadar˜am explains 35 figures. This author says that he follows DaıÒi in this chapter. 

<P>Poetry is compared to a lady decorated with apparel and ornaments. The words are the members of her body. The subject-matter is her life. The rhythmic variation (Vaııam) is her complexion. The style of language is her mode of gait. (1) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_144">Vi_144</A>
<BR>((Defectless words are faultless members.))</H3>


<P>144. The words used in poetry should signify explicitly. They must be Tamil words. If Sanskrit words are to be employed they should be Tamilised with due Tamil sound. They should have been used in verses composed by the great and learned of the past. There should be no ambiguity in the meaning. Only such defectless words are the members of the body of poetry. (2) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_145">Vi_145</A>
<BR>((Defective words are faulty members.))</H3>


<P>145. Such words that are used in unrefined speech, that do not possess melodious sound, that are having Sanskrit letters in them and that do not signify clearly without a say of ambiguity are defective words and so faulty members of the body of poetry. (3) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_146">Vi_146</A>
<BR>((Clear subject-matter is defectless life.))</H3>

 

<P>146. The subject-matter should rejoice the listener or the reader of the poetry. It should be informative profitable. It should be devoid of such defects as pointed out by scholars. It should be easily comprehensible. Such subject matter alone is deemed as defectless life of poetry. (4) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_147">Vi_147</A>
<BR>((Defective subject-matter is defective life.))</H3>


<P>147. The subject-matter that is not easily comprehensible to the reader or the listener of the poetry, that may be argued as defective and confusing and that gives room to controversial meanings and such others is deemed as defective life of poetry. (5) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_148">Vi_148</A>
<BR>((The subject-matter is the life of poetry.))</H3>


<P>148.     The words of poetry as well as its subject-matter should be flawless when only poetry will have figures. If they are otherwise poetry will prove lifeless. The life of poetry abides in its subject-matter. Thus spoke the author, DaıÒi. (6) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_149">Vi_149</A>
<BR>((The tenfold life mentioned by the Vaitaruppar and KauÒar))</H3>


<P>149. The <i>cil„ÒÒam</i>, the <i>ut‡ratai</i>, the <i>pula≠</i>, the <i>camatai</i>, the <i>cam‡ti</i>, the <i>poruÎ-teÎivu</i>, the <i>ùcam</i>, the <i>cukum‡ratai</i> and the <i>i≠pam</i> are the inherent excellence of style in the poetic composition. These are called by the <i>vaitaruppar</i> tenfold life of poetry. 

<P>The <i>kauÒar</i> have controversial explanations for all these ten merits. (7) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_150">Vi_150</A>
& <A NAME="_Vi_trsl_151">Vi_151</A>
<BR>((Cil„ÒÒam, etc. defined.))</H3>


<P>150 & 151.  
<UL>
<LI>1. <i>cil„ÒÒam</i> --- The compactness of the metrical feet. 

<LI>2. <i>ùcam</i> --- Omission as of an inflectional sign in combination of words. 

<LI>3. <i>camatai</i> --- The quality of having equal number of letters in all lines. 

<LI>4. <i>cam‡ti</i> --- Attribution of a deed to a person or thing other than its agent. 

<LI>5. <i>cukum‡ratai</i> --- Smoothness of expression attained by the use of soft consonants. 

<LI>6. <i>k‡nti</i> --- Description within conventional limits satisfying the aesthetic sense. 

<LI>7. <i>ut‡ram</i> --- Description of liberality of a person. 

<LI>8. <i>pula≠</i> --- Clarity of meaning by known words. 

<LI>9. <i>poruÎ-teÎivu</i> --- The meaning becoming clear through the context that is understandable by means of a word or phrase in the verse. 

<LI>10. <i>i≠pam</i> --- Sweetness of subject-matter and of style and diction. (8), (9)
</UL>


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_152">Vi_152</A>
<BR>((<i>ta≠mai</i>, <i>uvamai</i>, <i>uruvakam</i>, <i>t„pakam</i> and <i>m„Òci</i>))</H3>


<P>152.     In this <i>k‡rikai</i> five figures are listed each with short definition. 
<UL>
<LI><i>ta≠mai</i> --- This figure consists in describing an object as it is. It is fourfold, namely, the object, the species, the action and the quality. 
<LI><i>uvamai</i> --- This figure consists in comparing one with the other. 
<LI><i>uruvakam</i> --- This figure consists in speaking the compared and the comparison as one without separation. 
<LI><i>d„vakam</i> --- This figure consists in linking a word with other words preceding or succeeding in the beginning, middle or end of a verse. 
<LI><i>m„Òci</i> --- In this figure a same word or a same meaning or both are repeated (thrice or more). (10)
</UL>


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_153">Vi_153</A>
<BR>((**))</H3>

<P>153.     In this <i>k‡rikai</i> ten figures are listed: 1) <i>piüaperuÎ-vaippu</i> 2) <i>taÒai-mo©i</i> 3) <i>vitirõkam</i> 4) <i>vip‡vanai</i> 5) <i>curukku</i> 6) <i>perukku</i> 7) <i>nùkkam</i> 8) <i>õtu</i> 9) <i>nu≠ukkam</i> and 10) <i>ilõcu</i>. (11) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_154">Vi_154</A>
<BR>((**))</H3>

<P>154. In this <i>k‡rikai</i> fifteen figures are listed:
<UL>
<LI>1) <i>aÒaivu</i> 
<LI>2) <i>maki©cci</i> 
<LI>3) <i>cuvai</i> 
<LI>4) <i>Âkkam</i> 
<LI>5) <i>pariy‡yamo©i</i> 
<LI>6) <i>tu≠aippõüu</i> 
<LI>7) <i>uÒattam</i> 
<LI>8) <i>avanuti</i> 
<LI>9) <i>cilõÒai</i> 
<LI>10) <i>ciüappu</i> 
<LI>11) <i>uÒanilai-c-col</i> 
<LI>12) <i>mura≠</i> 
<LI>13) <i>nuval‡-c-col</i> 
<LI>14) <i>terivil-puka©cci</i> 
<LI>and 15) <i>cuÒÒu</i>. (12)
</UL>



<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_155">Vi_155</A>
<BR>((**))</H3>

<P>155.     In this <i>k‡rikai</i>, among thirtyfive figures mentioned by DaıÒi, the remaining five are listed:
<UL>
<LI>1) <i>oruÔkiyal</i> 
<LI>2) <i>parim‡üüu</i> 
<LI>3) <i>‡ci</i> 
<LI>4) <i>vir‡vu</i> 
<LI>and <i>p‡vikam</i>.
</UL>

<P>There are authors who elaborate figures into many more. But this author follows only DaıÒi. (13) 



<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_156">Vi_156</A>
& <A NAME="_Vi_trsl_157">Vi_157</A>
<BR>((**))</H3>

<P>156&157. These two <i>k‡rikais</i> give a list of thirtytwo kinds of <i>uvamai</i>. The commentary explains them with illustrative examples. (14), (15) 



<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_158">Vi_158</A>
<BR>((The general definition of <i>uvamai</i>))</H3>


<P>158. In a simile which is <i>uvamai</i> an object is compared to another either in the natural state or in praising or in deriding. The simile has four divisions, namely, the object of comparison, the particle of comparison, the object compared and the point of comparison between them both. 

<P>The comparison takes place consequent on quality, result, action, form, etc. (16) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_159">Vi_159</A>
<BR>((**))</H3>

<P>159. Twelve, other kinds of simile are listed in this <i>k‡rikai</i>. (17) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_160">Vi_160</A>
& <A NAME="_Vi_trsl_161">Vi_161</A>
<BR>((**))</H3>

<P>160 & 161. Twenty one kinds of <i>uruvakam</i> are listed in this <i>k‡rikai</i>. (18), (19) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_162">Vi_162</A>
<BR>((<i>PiüaporuÎ-vaippu-aıi</i>))</H3>


<P>162. This is a figure in which a particular notion is substanciated by a general notion and vice versa. This is of eight kinds. (20) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_163">Vi_163</A>
& <A NAME="_Vi_trsl_164">Vi_164</A>
<BR>((<i>TaÒai-mo©i-aıi</i> ))</H3>

<P>163 & 164 This is a figure in which a statement is heightened in effect by the suggestion of an apparent contradiction. This is of thirty kinds. (21), (22) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_165">Vi_165</A>
<BR>((<i>Vitirika-aıi</i>))</H3>


<P>165.     This is a figure in which the difference between the object of comparison and the object compared is mentioned either explicitly or suggestively. (23) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_166">Vi_166</A>
<BR>((<i>Vip‡va≠ai-aıi</i> and <i>curukku-aıi</i>))</H3>


<P>166.  <i>Vip‡va≠ai</i> is a figure in which the effects are represented as taking place although their usual causes are absent. It is of four kinds. 

<P><i>Curukku</i> is a figure in which the idea of a subject-matter is sought to be conveyed suggestively by the description of another subject-matter resembling it. It is of four kinds. (24) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_167">Vi_167</A>
<BR>((<i>Perukku-aıi</i> and <i>nùkkam-aıi</i>))</H3>


<P>167. <i>Perukku</i> is hyperbole. It is of five kinds. 

<P><i>Nùkkam</i> is a figure which consists in the qualities and actions of an object being ascribed to another which is entirely different in nature. It is of two kinds. (25) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_168">Vi_168</A>
<BR>((<i>útu-aıi</i>))</H3>


<P>168. <i>útu</i> is a figure of speech which brings together the cause and effect. It is of ten kinds. (26) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_169">Vi_169</A>
<BR>((<i>Nu≠ukkam-aıi</i> and <i>ilõca-aıi</i>))</H3>

 

<P>169. <i>Nu≠ukkam</i> is a figure which expresses an idea by implication. It is of two kinds. 

<P><i>Ilõca</i> is a figure in which the physical expressions of an emotion in one's mind is deliberately attributed to some quite different cause or purpose, to conceal the emotion altogether. It is also defined as a figure in which what on all hands is considered as an advantage is represented; with a deprecatory air, as a disadvantage and vice versa. In other words, it consists in praising in the disguise of degradation and deriding in the disguise of praise. It is also of two kinds. (27) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_170">Vi_170</A>
<BR>((<i>Niral-nirai, maki©cci, cuvai, Âkkam</i> and <i>pariy‡yam</i>))</H3>


<P>170. <i>Niral-nirai</i> is also called <i>aÒaivu-aıi</i>. It is a figure in which words or phrases are arranged in different sets so that each term of one set may qualify or govern the corresponding term in the other.

<P><i>Maki©cci</i> is a figure in which impassioned language is employed to give vent to one's intensive love. 

<P><i>Cuvai</i> is a figure which consists in describing the eightfold sentiment as well as the calmness of mind. Therefore it is of nine kinds. 

<P><i>Êkkam</i> is a figure in which a person praises himself. 

<P><i>Pariy‡yam</i> is a figure in which one's view is not expresses by words but is cleverly suggested. (28) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_171">Vi_171</A>
<BR>((<i>Tu≠ai-p-põüu-aıi, ut‡tta-aıi</i> and <i>avanuti-aıi</i>))</H3>


<P>171. <i>Tu≠ai-p-põüu</i> or <i>cam‡yitam</i> is a figure in which an effort is described as proceeding from something other than its natural cause.

<P><i>Ut‡ttam</i> is a figure which expresses either abundance of wealth or exaltedness of thought. It is, therefore, of two kinds. 

<P><i>Avanuti</i> is a figure which denies to an object one of its own attributes and ascribes another which is foreign to it. It is of four kinds. (29) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_172">Vi_172</A>
<BR>((<i>CileÒai-aıi</i>))</H3>

 

<P>172. <i>CileÒai</i> which is paronomasia is a figure in which a single word or compound admits two or more interpretations. It is of seven kinds. (30) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_173">Vi_173</A>
<BR>((<i>Ciüappu-aıi, uÒanilai-aıi</i> and <i>muraı-aıi</i>))</H3>


<P>173. <i>Ciüappu</i> is a figure in which the excellence of an object is emphasised by describing it as lacking even in requisites or necessary attributes. It is of five kinds. 

<P><i>Uta≠ilai-c-col</i> is a figure in which an object that has to be commended for its merit or condemned for its demerit is mentioned along with several objects having the similar excellence or baseness, as the case may be, so as to render the comparison effective and telling. It is, therefore, of two kinds. 

<P><i>Muraı</i> is a figure in which antithesis or opposition in words and sense (in attributes and the attributed) occurs. It is therefore of three kinds. (31) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_174">Vi_174</A>
<BR>((<i>Nuval‡-c-col-aıi, terivil-puka©cci-aıi</i> and <i>cuÒÒu-aıi</i>))</H3>


<P>174. <i>Nuval‡-c-col</i> is a figure of speech which consists in formally praising a person or thing implying thereby disparagement of another person or thing. 

<P><i>Terivil-puka©cci</i> is a figure containing praise couched in words of apparent censure. <i>CuÒÒu</i> is a figure in which the quality or action of an object is described as affecting another, either for good or evil. It is therefore of two kinds. (32) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_175">Vi_175</A>
<BR>((<i>OruÔkiyal-aıi, parim‡üüa-aıi</i> and <i>A§ci-aıi</i>))</H3>


<P>175. <i>OruÔkiyal</i> is a figure in which two subjects are referred to one common predicate of action or attribute. It is therefore, two kinds. 

<P><i>Parim‡üüam</i> is a figure describing an exchange.

<P><i>A§ci</i> is a figure expressing benediction. (33) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_176">Vi_176</A>
<BR>((<i>Viravu-aıi</i> and <i>p‡vikam-aıi</i>))</H3>


<P>176. <i>Viravu</i> is a composite figure where several figures are blended (with equal merit). 

<P><i>P‡vikam</i> is the basic ethics running through a poem (34) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_177">Vi_177</A>
<BR>((General permissive rule regarding the figures))</H3>


<P>177.  Scholars elaborate the kinds in various figures which may also be taken notice of. (35) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_178">Vi_178</A>
<BR>((The kinds of verses))</H3>


<P>178. Verses are classified into four, namely, the <i>muttakam</i>, the <i>kuÎakam</i>, the <i>tokai-nilai</i> and the <i>k‡ppiyam</i>. 

<P><i>Muttakam</i> is a stanza which by itself is complete in sense. 

<P><i>KuÎakam</i> is the combination of two, three or more stanzas having a single finite verb in order to complete the sense; occasionally a nominal predicate is also met with.

<P><i>Tokai-nilai</i> is an anthology of poems collected on certain definite principles. There may be one author or many. 

<P><i>K‡ppiyam</i> is the narrative poem or epic poem, may it be long or short. (36) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_179">Vi_179</A>
<BR>((<i>Collaıi, MaÒakku</i>))</H3>

 

<P>179. <i>Collaıi</i> is a figure of speech depending for its effect on verbal sound alone. 

<P><i>MaÒakku </i>is the repetition of a word, foot or line in a stanza, giving a different meaning each time. It is of various kinds, namely repetition in the beginning, repetition in the end, repetition in the middle, repetition in the beginning and middle, repetition in the beginning and end, repetition in the middle and end, and repetition in all the three places. (37) 

 

<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_180">Vi_180</A>
<BR>((The opinion of others))</H3>

 

<P>180. The same authors are of the opinion that <i>tantira-utti</i>, <i>tantira-kuıam</i>, <i>tantira-matam</i>, <i>tantira-urai</i>, the means of acquiring correct knowledge of an object mentioned in logic which are cause, illustration, proof, etc. and <i>cittira-kavi</i> are also figures of speech. You are required to know them also. 

<UL>
<LI>1. <i>Tantira-utti</i> --- The literary devices employed by an author of a standard work, who keeps in mind the rule of exegesis. They are 32 in number. 
<LI>2. <i>Tantira-kuıam</i> --- The beauties or excellences of a treatise being ten in number.
<LI>3. <i>Tantira-matam</i> --- The seven kinds of attitude of an author towards a certain subject. 
<LI>4. <i>Tantira-urai</i> --- The fourteen requisites of interpretation. 
<LI>5.<i> Piram‡≠am</i> which is the means of acquiring correct knowledge of a thing. They are eight, namely,
<UL>
<LI><i>pirattiyaÒcam</i>, 
<LI><i>a≠um‡ıam</i>, 
<LI><i>‡kamam</i>, 
<LI><i>uvam‡≠am</i>, 
<LI><i>arutt‡patti</i>, 
<LI><i>a≠upalatti</i>, 
<LI><i>campavam</i> 
<LI>and <i>aitikam</i>. 
</UL>
<LI>6. <i>Cittira-kavi</i> --- A variety of metrical composition fitted into fanciful pictures, etc. (38) 
</UL>


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_181">Vi_181</A>
<BR>((<i>Cittira-kavi</i>))</H3>

<P>181. <i>Cittira-kavi</i> is manifold some of which are <i>m‡lai-m‡üüu</i>, <i>cakkaram</i>, <i>ùr-i≠a-p-p‡ÒÒu</i>, <i>ùr-e©uttu-p-p‡ÒÒu</i>, <i>vi≠‡-uttaram</i>, <i>õka-p‡tam</i>, <i>k‡tai-karappu</i>, <i>cu©ikuÎam</i>, <i>cittira-p-p‡</i> and <i>kùmÂttiri</i>. 

<UL>
<LI><i>m‡lai-m‡üüu</i> --- A kind of verse that remains identical where its letters are read in reverse direction.))</H3>

<LI><i>cakkaram</i> --- A fantastic metrical composition in the diagrammatic form of a cart-wheel. 


<LI><i>ùri≠app‡ÒÒu</i> ---  A stanza of two or more lines made of either hard consonants and hard consonant-vowels alone or soft consonants and soft consonant-vowels alone or medial consonants and medial consonant-vowels alone. 

<LI><i>ùre©uttupp‡ÒÒu</i> --- A stanza of two or more lines made of a single consonant and its corresponding vowel-consonants alone. 


<LI><i>vi≠‡-uttaram</i> ---  A kind of poetical composition in which a word having a particular significance is split into a number of words, each serving as a reply to each question set before. 


<LI><i>õka-p‡tam</i> --- A stanza of four lines all apparently alike but really made up of different sets of words and so conveying different meanings. 


<LI><i>k‡tai-karappu</i> --- A stanza so composed that by beginning with the letter of the last word reading from the end every alternate letter only, a new stanza of different metre is formed. 


<LI><i>cu©ikuÎam</i> --- A four-lined stanza, each line consisting of eight letters, so composed that the letters are arranged in a series of incurving loops one within another. 


<LI><i>cittira-p-p‡</i> --- A curiously wrought poem, the composition of which is not clear for want of illustration. 


<LI><i>kùmÂttiri</i> --- A verse of two lines composed in such a way that the first letter of the first line, the second letter of the second line, the third letter of the first line, the fourth letter of the second line and so on when read together form the first line. The first letter of the second line, the second letter of the first line, the third letter of the second line, the fourth letter of the first line and so on form the second line.
</UL>

<P>Certain other kinds of <i>cittira-kavi</i> are explained in the commentary. They are
<UL>
<LI><i>e©ukÂüüirukkai</i>, 
<LI><i>k‡rantuüai-p‡ÒÒu</i>, 
<LI><i>tÂcaÔ-koÎal</i>,  
<LI><i>v‡val-§‡üüu</i>, 
<LI><i>p‡ta-mayakku</i>, 
<LI><i>oüüu-p-peyarttal</i>, 
<LI><i>caruppato-pattiram</i>, etc. (39)[5]
</UL>


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_182">Vi_182</A>
<BR>((Vir‡viyal and Maıippirav‡Îam, etc.))</H3>


<P>182. If the Sanskrit letters mingle in the words and phrases of Tamil, that type of (poetical) composition is called Vir‡viyal. If Sanskrit words mingle in the sentences of Tamil, that way of composition is called Maıi-p-pirav‡Îam. Tuüai-k-kavi are verses on themes connected with Akam and Puüam. PiraÎikai are verses on riddle. (40) 


<H3><A NAME="_Vi_trsl_183">Vi_183</A>
<BR>((<i>Tuüai-k-kavi, kiÎavi-k-kavi</i> and the uses of poetry))</H3>

 

<P>183. <i>Maüam, kaÎi, tÂtu, vayirapam, campiratam, tavacu kuüam, kaıikam</i>, etc. are the themes of <i>k‡mpakam</i>, a kind of poem. They are called <i>tuüai-k-kavi</i>. <i>KiÎavi-k-kavi</i> are the themes found in amatory compositions. The uses of poetry are performing virtuous deeds, earning wealth, enjoying worldly pleasures and attaining salvation at the end. (41) 

 

<H1>V„racù©iyam ends.</H1>

<HR>

<H1>The <i>k‡rikai</i> about the commentator.</H1>


<P>The grammar work V„racù©iyam is a composition of 181 <i>k‡rikai</i> verses by the chief of Po≠paüüi, girt with many tanks and groves. In order to help those who study Tamil with intention, Peruntõva≠ has written paraphrase with explanations to all the 181 <i>k‡rikai</i>, taking it his duty. His commentary has the worth to be praised by the world. 

 

<P>[V„racù©iyam is a book of 183 verses of which 181 are the composition of the author, Puttamittira≠‡r. Two verses of Ve≠p‡ at the end of the chapter on words are later additions. The commentator has explained those two verses too.] 


<HR>

<H1>Notes</H1>


<P>[1] During the period of V„racù©iyam <i>e õ</i> and <i>o ù</i> were represented respectively by one letter only (<i>e</i> and <i>o</i>). Short??? <i>e</i> and <i>o</i> were represented by a dot on each letter.

<P>[2] The rule for <i>≠</i> is made the rule for © also on account of the non-perception of the difference in sound between the two letters. This strange rule is found and maintained only in this work.

<P>[3] The above rule relating to <i>©</i> is found only in this book.

<P>[4] Note: Here strict adherence to the established tradition is a must.

<P>[5] The definitions found mostly in this chapter are adopted from Tamil Lexicon of Madras University.

